what that means.

663
00:26:29,488 --> 00:26:32,958
- You know, I like it, honey,
I think we should make an offer.

664
00:26:32,992 --> 00:26:34,660
- He's around.

665
00:26:34,693 --> 00:26:35,762
- Where?

666
00:26:35,795 --> 00:26:37,630
- I'm in the outhouse,
I'll be right back out.

667
00:26:37,663 --> 00:26:39,967
- ...getting more water
for these potatoes.

668
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:42,002
- So, he's Irish, huh?

669
00:26:42,035 --> 00:26:43,068
- Uh-huh.
So what do we do?

670
00:26:43,102 --> 00:26:44,737
- We could start the salad.

671
00:26:44,770 --> 00:26:45,905
- Wait in there.

672
00:26:45,938 --> 00:26:47,807
- Cover me,
I'll set the table.

673
00:26:50,210 --> 00:26:53,313
- We'll hide behind this tree,
are you sure you're the best?

674
00:26:54,813 --> 00:26:56,115
- Alright now,
how do you want him?

675
00:26:56,148 --> 00:26:57,383
- Lady's choice.

676
00:26:57,417 --> 00:26:58,385
- Alive.

677
00:26:58,418 --> 00:27:00,253
- Why, how do you want him?

678
00:27:00,287 --> 00:27:02,722
- Where do you come from,
Mister Miro?

679
00:27:02,755 --> 00:27:04,690
- Lately, mostly from
around Tombstone.

680
00:27:04,723 --> 00:27:06,225
- Before that,
Medford, Wisconsin.

681
00:27:06,258 --> 00:27:08,027
- Pretty rough place.

682
00:27:08,060 --> 00:27:10,297
- That's what they tell me.

683
00:27:10,330 --> 00:27:12,732
- Still trying to figure out
where I've heard of you.

684
00:27:12,765 --> 00:27:14,467
- It'll come.

685
00:27:14,500 --> 00:27:16,435
- Were you in the war?

686
00:27:16,468 --> 00:27:18,103
- Bragg's Army.
- [indistinct].

687
00:27:18,137 --> 00:27:19,838
- That was a long time ago.

688
00:27:19,872 --> 00:27:21,307
- Not according
to our good Mayor.

689
00:27:21,340 --> 00:27:23,275
For him, the South
is still winning.

690
00:27:23,308 --> 00:27:24,977
- Gideon Polk?

691
00:27:25,012 --> 00:27:26,947
- You know him?

692
00:27:26,980 --> 00:27:29,148
- Captain Polk and I are
old Army friends.

693
00:27:29,182 --> 00:27:30,917
- Okay, let's just focus on
our stakeout right now.

694
00:27:30,950 --> 00:27:33,787
- He's my main reason
for coming to Oracle,

695
00:27:33,820 --> 00:27:35,555
except maybe now
I found a better one.

696
00:27:35,588 --> 00:27:36,522
[Horse whinnies]

697
00:27:36,555 --> 00:27:38,190
- Hey, it's Bigfoot
with a bobkin.

698
00:27:39,892 --> 00:27:42,062
- Well, folks, we're going
to [indistinct] too.

699
00:27:43,663 --> 00:27:45,332
- Nice day.
- Nice day.

700
00:27:45,365 --> 00:27:47,767
- I'm arresting you
for the murder of Uriah Baker.

701
00:27:47,800 --> 00:27:49,902
Drop your gun belt.

702
00:27:49,935 --> 00:27:51,170
- I said your gun belt.

703
00:27:51,203 --> 00:27:52,472
- I'd also like to have
a little talk with you

704
00:27:52,505 --> 00:27:54,473
about your connection
with Erica Page.

705
00:27:55,775 --> 00:27:57,343
- I'm gonna talk,
I'm gonna ride.

706
00:27:57,377 --> 00:27:59,478
- Keep your mouth shut.

707
00:27:59,512 --> 00:28:00,880
I'll keep him covered, Marshal.

708
00:28:00,913 --> 00:28:02,748
How 'bout your
dousing the fire.

709
00:28:02,782 --> 00:28:05,952
- You know, the way you do,
I like that.

710
00:28:08,455 --> 00:28:09,422
[Gunshot]

711
00:28:09,455 --> 00:28:11,657
- I was just going to get
your potato.

712
00:28:11,690 --> 00:28:13,058
- That happened faster
than it should have.

713
00:28:13,092 --> 00:28:14,527
- Over the speed of light.

714
00:28:14,560 --> 00:28:15,562
- Yes, ma'am.

715
00:28:16,997 --> 00:28:18,498
Come on.

716
00:28:18,532 --> 00:28:21,000
I'll help you get your
worm bait back to beyond.

717
00:28:21,033 --> 00:28:22,235
[♪♪♪]

718
00:28:22,268 --> 00:28:25,238
- Yes, stop, quiet,
Charlie, oh...

719
00:28:26,973 --> 00:28:29,742
- Yes, bunch of steel
of the Old West.

720
00:28:29,775 --> 00:28:32,212
- And naughty spank
and naughty spunk,

721
00:28:32,245 --> 00:28:34,280
and you've been
back in the touché.

722
00:28:34,313 --> 00:28:36,415
Now, naughty,
naughty, naughty

723
00:28:36,448 --> 00:28:39,218
and naughty, naughty,
naughty this way,

724
00:28:39,252 --> 00:28:40,987
naughty, naughty,
naughty,

725
00:28:41,020 --> 00:28:43,523
and naughty, naughty,
naughty, that way.

726
00:28:43,557 --> 00:28:45,825
Hiya, girls.

727
00:28:45,858 --> 00:28:48,393
- Say what you want, Reverend,
this brings in the parishioners.

728
00:28:51,230 --> 00:28:53,633
- Oh, look at them
poor girls there.

729
00:28:53,667 --> 00:28:55,168
- Do you know
that Mrs. Marshal Hood's

730
00:28:55,202 --> 00:28:57,137
ordered them out of town?

731
00:28:57,137 --> 00:28:59,238
She says they constitute
an "immoral influence"

732
00:28:59,272 --> 00:29:00,307
on our young men.

733
00:29:00,340 --> 00:29:01,373
[Laughing]

734
00:29:01,407 --> 00:29:03,142
- That's pretty much
the idea, isn't it?

735
00:29:03,175 --> 00:29:05,512
[♪♪♪]

736
00:29:05,545 --> 00:29:08,815
- Joel, you know, it's so good
you almost forget it's erotic.

737
00:29:08,848 --> 00:29:11,050
- ♪ Shuffle Off the Buffalo
Shuffle Off the Buffalo ♪

738
00:29:11,083 --> 00:29:12,218
♪ Then stick the butt up... ♪

739
00:29:12,252 --> 00:29:13,920
- Yeah, I remember,
when we were part of the act,

740
00:29:13,953 --> 00:29:18,257
they said
I didn't glide quite right.

741
00:29:18,290 --> 00:29:19,258
[Applause]

742
00:29:19,292 --> 00:29:20,993
- Hear they gotta be out
by Friday.

743
00:29:21,027 --> 00:29:23,797
- Friday noon.
- Whatever.

744
00:29:23,830 --> 00:29:26,665
- Somebody's always tryin' to
push somebody else out.

745
00:29:26,698 --> 00:29:28,535
Like the Yankees trying to
push us out of Georgia.

746
00:29:28,568 --> 00:29:29,668
TOGETHER:
Shut up.

747
00:29:29,702 --> 00:29:31,237
- Looks to me
they accomplished that.

748
00:29:31,270 --> 00:29:33,573
- Not at first, they didn't.

749
00:29:33,607 --> 00:29:34,607
You should've been with me at...

750
00:29:34,640 --> 00:29:36,008
- Excuse me, who is talking?

751
00:29:36,042 --> 00:29:38,845
- ...smash Grant and Gould's
plans at the Doodlebug.

752
00:29:38,878 --> 00:29:39,845
- Googleberg?

753
00:29:39,878 --> 00:29:41,780
- Let's see, uh,
you were in the artillery

754
00:29:41,815 --> 00:29:42,948
by then, weren't you?

755
00:29:42,982 --> 00:29:44,417
- Yes, sir.

756
00:29:44,450 --> 00:29:45,618
- You're a bootleg.

757
00:29:45,652 --> 00:29:48,522
- ...shot rounds and canisters
as fast as we could load.

758
00:29:48,555 --> 00:29:49,522
Why, we handled them guns

759
00:29:49,555 --> 00:29:51,723
like they was
red-hot stovepipes.

760
00:29:51,757 --> 00:29:52,725
- How well did you handle them

761
00:29:52,758 --> 00:29:54,527
at Lookout Mountain,
Captain Polk?

762
00:29:54,560 --> 00:29:56,662
- Hell, I don't recall,

763
00:29:56,695 --> 00:29:58,097
I don't recall.

764
00:29:58,130 --> 00:30:00,533
- Mr. Mooneys of the Old West.

765
00:30:00,567 --> 00:30:01,835
- Why, Mister Cane Miro.

766
00:30:03,302 --> 00:30:04,270
It is you.

767
00:30:05,405 --> 00:30:07,373
- You've got a good memory.

768
00:30:07,407 --> 00:30:09,142
But then, that was something
no one could forget,

769
00:30:09,175 --> 00:30:10,243
wasn't it, Captain?

770
00:30:10,277 --> 00:30:11,443
- That he's got a good memory.

771
00:30:11,477 --> 00:30:13,012
- I thought you were dead.

772
00:30:13,045 --> 00:30:14,413
- The good die first.

773
00:30:14,447 --> 00:30:16,115
- Most people are
morally ambiguous,

774
00:30:16,148 --> 00:30:18,350
which explains our random
dying patterns.

775
00:30:18,383 --> 00:30:20,853
- I understand
you're a good citizen.

776
00:30:20,887 --> 00:30:22,288
- Now that's a complex
death threat.

777
00:30:22,322 --> 00:30:23,657
- Why'd you come here?

778
00:30:23,690 --> 00:30:25,192
- Well, goodnight, Mayor.

779
00:30:25,225 --> 00:30:26,192
Goodnight, Erica.

780
00:30:26,225 --> 00:30:27,360
We'll wind up
our business tomorrow.

781
00:30:27,393 --> 00:30:28,795
- Let me call you a cab.

782
00:30:28,828 --> 00:30:29,795
- Don't leave.

783
00:30:29,828 --> 00:30:32,132
- Are you afraid, Captain?

784
00:30:32,165 --> 00:30:34,867
How can one man help you
when 30,000 couldn't?

785
00:30:34,900 --> 00:30:36,102
- He makes a good point.

786
00:30:36,135 --> 00:30:37,937
- Hello, Cane.

787
00:30:37,970 --> 00:30:39,172
- Hello, Erica.

788
00:30:40,373 --> 00:30:41,473
It's been a long time.

789
00:30:41,507 --> 00:30:43,508
- It's been a long, long,
long, long lonely time.

790
00:30:43,543 --> 00:30:44,677
- I wouldn't touch 'em.

791
00:30:44,710 --> 00:30:47,680
- With all that makeup.
- Come on with me, Gideon.

792
00:30:47,713 --> 00:30:50,115
- You better retreat, Mayor.

793
00:30:50,148 --> 00:30:52,652
I'll see you another day.

794
00:30:52,685 --> 00:30:54,120
- Goodnight, Cane.

795
00:30:54,153 --> 00:30:55,120
Goodnight, Erica.

796
00:30:55,153 --> 00:30:56,122
- Boy, the Mayor's
approval rating

797
00:30:56,155 --> 00:30:58,057
just plunged like 20 points.

798
00:30:58,090 --> 00:30:59,058
- Oh.

799
00:30:59,092 --> 00:31:00,192
- What was that all about?

800
00:31:00,227 --> 00:31:01,895
- You know, it's tough,
we used to be roommates.

801
00:31:01,928 --> 00:31:03,195
- It's a long story.

802
00:31:03,228 --> 00:31:05,798
- Well, leave him alone.
I need him.

803
00:31:05,832 --> 00:31:07,433
- So do I.

804
00:31:07,467 --> 00:31:09,602
Now tell me
why you sent for me.

805
00:31:09,635 --> 00:31:12,005
Your friend here said something
about a killing.

806
00:31:12,038 --> 00:31:13,138
- Who needs me?

807
00:31:13,172 --> 00:31:14,940
Who needs Wormy?

808
00:31:14,973 --> 00:31:16,108
- We have to talk shop here?

809
00:31:16,142 --> 00:31:18,343
- Old Western art
of the Old West.

810
00:31:18,377 --> 00:31:20,045
- Come on into
my dressing room.

811
00:31:23,115 --> 00:31:25,618
- And I don't like him.

812
00:31:28,922 --> 00:31:31,823
- That's a line that Miss Kitty
and Matthew never crossed.

813
00:31:31,857 --> 00:31:33,458
- Jake was right.

814
00:31:33,492 --> 00:31:34,593
A killing.

815
00:31:36,628 --> 00:31:37,597
Now, kiss me, Cane.

816
00:31:37,630 --> 00:31:38,698
- My co-partner.

817
00:31:38,732 --> 00:31:39,965
- Make it like old times.

818
00:31:39,998 --> 00:31:41,400
- Well, you mean,
like after the football game

819
00:31:41,433 --> 00:31:44,303
up by the dumb...

820
00:31:44,337 --> 00:31:45,872
- You got a wood kick there,
let me get the match.

821
00:31:45,905 --> 00:31:48,007
- Who do I kill?
The brave Captain?

822
00:31:48,040 --> 00:31:49,142
- Is he getting shorter?

823
00:31:49,175 --> 00:31:52,512
- Jake, this man has
no romance in his soul.

824
00:31:52,545 --> 00:31:54,113
Maybe you kill nobody.

825
00:31:54,147 --> 00:31:56,148
- I didn't come up here just
to make it like old times.

826
00:31:56,182 --> 00:31:59,918
- Oh.
- And I get paid in advance.

827
00:31:59,952 --> 00:32:02,455
- Jake, get 3,000
out of the office safe.

828
00:32:02,488 --> 00:32:03,757
Take your time doing that.

829
00:32:03,790 --> 00:32:06,292
- If looks could kill you,
you'd be so almost dead.

830
00:32:06,325 --> 00:32:07,993
[Door slams]

831
00:32:08,027 --> 00:32:09,328
- That's better.

832
00:32:09,362 --> 00:32:10,730
No point
in my having to trust you

833
00:32:10,763 --> 00:32:13,398
when I can worry
about nicer things.

834
00:32:13,432 --> 00:32:14,600
- What things, Cane?

835
00:32:14,633 --> 00:32:17,403
- Raindrops on roses,
whiskers on kittens.

836
00:32:17,437 --> 00:32:21,073
- ...who do I kill
and when?

837
00:32:21,107 --> 00:32:22,342
- The local law.

838
00:32:22,375 --> 00:32:24,510
- Oh, get a room...
oh, they did.

839
00:32:24,543 --> 00:32:26,878
- Hmm, chocolaty.

840
00:32:26,912 --> 00:32:29,715
- Why wait 'til Saturday?

841
00:32:29,748 --> 00:32:31,283
- On Saturday,
the Mayor gets a letter

842
00:32:31,317 --> 00:32:33,518
from the railroad
Board of Directors

843
00:32:33,552 --> 00:32:34,520
letting the town know

844
00:32:34,553 --> 00:32:35,855
if the new tracks
come through here.

845
00:32:35,888 --> 00:32:36,990
- Is that a euphemism?

846
00:32:37,023 --> 00:32:38,157
- If they do, I clean up.

847
00:32:38,190 --> 00:32:39,258
- And I think
that's a euphemism.

848
00:32:39,292 --> 00:32:41,793
- Maybe you won't
have to kill Mrs. Hood.

849
00:32:41,827 --> 00:32:42,828
If they don't,
I'll have to grab

850
00:32:42,862 --> 00:32:45,365
all the money I can
and clear out fast.

851
00:32:45,398 --> 00:32:46,432
There'll be killings.

852
00:32:46,465 --> 00:32:48,000
- This is more
than I want to know.

853
00:32:48,033 --> 00:32:50,135
- I want the Marshal
to be the first to die.

854
00:32:50,168 --> 00:32:52,605
- Oh, yeah, give me
more exposition.

855
00:32:52,638 --> 00:32:54,240
- ...if you like.

856
00:32:54,273 --> 00:32:56,375
- Thanks for the bonus.

857
00:32:56,408 --> 00:32:58,010
So much for business.

858
00:32:58,043 --> 00:32:59,545
Let's get down to pleasure.

859
00:32:59,578 --> 00:33:00,880
- Some bonus.

860
00:33:03,148 --> 00:33:05,885
- Well, got down to pleasure,
quite pleasurable.

861
00:33:05,918 --> 00:33:07,720
- Remember me?

862
00:33:07,753 --> 00:33:09,955
- You were on Carter Country,
weren't you?

863
00:33:11,257 --> 00:33:12,392
- Cane Miro.

864
00:33:12,425 --> 00:33:14,493
Wanted in three states
and five territories

865
00:33:14,527 --> 00:33:16,295
for murder,
bank robbery, stage robbery,

866
00:33:16,328 --> 00:33:18,430
train robbery
and sundry assault.

867
00:33:18,463 --> 00:33:19,398
- Neat.

868
00:33:19,432 --> 00:33:20,433
- Take a look
at your ledger.

869
00:33:20,467 --> 00:33:21,633
See if I'm wanted
in this state.

870
00:33:21,667 --> 00:33:22,802
I haven't checked lately.

871
00:33:22,835 --> 00:33:23,970
- My computer is down.

872
00:33:24,003 --> 00:33:24,970
[Clacking]

873
00:33:25,003 --> 00:33:26,372
- You ought to
keep these clean.

874
00:33:27,940 --> 00:33:30,042
- No, you're not wanted here.

875
00:33:30,075 --> 00:33:31,410
If you were,
I'd find a place for you

876
00:33:31,443 --> 00:33:32,778
with the other characters
I met this week.

877
00:33:32,812 --> 00:33:35,448
- You wipe your nose.
- ...my husband on Boot Hill.

878
00:33:35,482 --> 00:33:36,482
- So, you're single?

879
00:33:36,515 --> 00:33:38,350
- Veterans sleep together
like kinfolk.

880
00:33:38,383 --> 00:33:39,685
- Not my kinfolk.

881
00:33:39,718 --> 00:33:41,587
- ...difference between 'em.

882
00:33:41,620 --> 00:33:43,823
- The bandits usually
get there first.

883
00:33:43,857 --> 00:33:45,858
- Everyone gets there
sooner or later.

884
00:33:45,892 --> 00:33:46,958
I'm not worried.

885
00:33:46,992 --> 00:33:48,660
- Anyway, the good die first,
you know.

886
00:33:48,693 --> 00:33:50,430
- I may get there
in the next five minutes.

887
00:33:51,697 --> 00:33:53,232
What's your business
in Oracle?

888
00:33:53,265 --> 00:33:54,367
- Herbal life.

889
00:33:54,400 --> 00:33:56,768
- It's honest.
It's also private.

890
00:33:56,802 --> 00:33:59,305
- Huh.
Well, that's no answer.

891
00:33:59,338 --> 00:34:00,807
- I intend to keep it
that way.

892
00:34:00,840 --> 00:34:02,575
- Not exactly a Howard Hawks
film, isn't it?

893
00:34:02,608 --> 00:34:04,610
- How long's it going to
take you to finish it up?

894
00:34:04,643 --> 00:34:06,345
- Ooh, about five weeks.

895
00:34:07,513 --> 00:34:08,748
- Make it five days?

896
00:34:08,782 --> 00:34:11,350
- What are they negotiating,
when he's going to kill her?

897
00:34:11,383 --> 00:34:13,820
- I might be able to finish
in five days,

898
00:34:13,853 --> 00:34:16,188
but that also gives me
five nights.

899
00:34:16,222 --> 00:34:17,490
What do you do nights?

900
00:34:17,523 --> 00:34:19,592
- Oh, I rut like
a crazed weasel, you?

901
00:34:19,625 --> 00:34:23,495
- Oh, I-- I keep an eye
on suspicious characters.

902
00:34:23,528 --> 00:34:25,832
- I'm a suspicious character.

903
00:34:25,865 --> 00:34:27,367
- I think I'll assign Joshua
to you.

904
00:34:27,400 --> 00:34:28,600
He takes care of drunks,

905
00:34:28,633 --> 00:34:30,368
mad dogs,
and smart saddle tramps.

906
00:34:30,402 --> 00:34:32,938
- And Englishmen.
- Then I'm a celebrity.

907
00:34:32,972 --> 00:34:35,040
You ought to give me
your personal attention.

908
00:34:35,073 --> 00:34:37,110
- I was up for
a CableACE Award.

909
00:34:37,143 --> 00:34:39,578
- I just hope I don't have to,
Mister Miro.

910
00:34:39,612 --> 00:34:41,680
- Well, I'm going to shut down
the sexual bantering

911
00:34:41,713 --> 00:34:43,015
for the night.

912
00:34:44,917 --> 00:34:48,153
- Oh boy, oh Beverly.

913
00:34:51,123 --> 00:34:52,558
- Is this is a video diary?

914
00:34:52,592 --> 00:34:53,692
- It's like a Cubert film.

915
00:34:53,725 --> 00:34:56,962
- No, no, no, it doesn't
seem like a Tuesday.

916
00:34:58,530 --> 00:35:00,633
- Oh, back to Monday,
May 31st.

917
00:35:00,667 --> 00:35:05,472
- ...ninety, ninety-five,
fifteen thousand.

918
00:35:05,505 --> 00:35:08,007
Well, it's all there.

919
00:35:08,040 --> 00:35:09,075
- Enjoy your timeshare.

920
00:35:09,108 --> 00:35:10,408
- I'll mail you
your first monthly payment

921
00:35:10,442 --> 00:35:11,843
after you get to Santa Fe.

922
00:35:11,877 --> 00:35:13,278
- You know, back then,
money was a lot of money.

923
00:35:13,345 --> 00:35:15,280
- Huh, now that
the deal is closed,

924
00:35:15,313 --> 00:35:16,248
I don't mind telling you

925
00:35:16,282 --> 00:35:18,583
that I got the best
of the bargain.

926
00:35:18,617 --> 00:35:20,318
A widened freight line
won't do you much good

927
00:35:20,352 --> 00:35:23,155
when the railroad comes,
and if it doesn't,

928
00:35:23,188 --> 00:35:27,460
this town will have the business
of a steamboat in Death Valley.

929
00:35:27,493 --> 00:35:28,660
- I'm funny.

930
00:35:28,693 --> 00:35:29,895
- Have you ever met a woman
with a head for business?

931
00:35:29,928 --> 00:35:31,297
- [CHUCKLING]
Afraid not.

932
00:35:31,330 --> 00:35:33,665
- Well, neither have I,
Mister Taft.

933
00:35:33,665 --> 00:35:34,733
Have a nice trip.

934
00:35:34,767 --> 00:35:36,568
- I'll be riding
the high road.

935
00:35:36,602 --> 00:35:38,603
- William Howard Taft,
ladies and gentlemen.

936
00:35:40,338 --> 00:35:42,275
- Nice of him to tell us
he's takin' the high road, huh?

937
00:35:42,308 --> 00:35:44,243
- It's a figure of speech,
Wormy.

938
00:35:44,277 --> 00:35:46,045
- I wish I could send
Cane on this.

939
00:35:46,078 --> 00:35:48,413
- He won't take
any of these puny jobs.

940
00:35:48,447 --> 00:35:51,383
Besides, why pay for something
I'll do for free?

941
00:35:51,417 --> 00:35:52,518
- Wormy, that's an O-Cedar.

942
00:35:52,552 --> 00:35:54,587
- If you ever get caught,
they'll come after me.

943
00:35:54,620 --> 00:35:56,422
That's why I try to keep you
from sin, little man.

944
00:35:56,455 --> 00:35:58,057
- Did you marry
the ketchup bottles?

945
00:35:58,090 --> 00:35:59,292
[Crash]

946
00:35:59,325 --> 00:36:01,960
- Cane Miro's mighty handy
with the women, isn't he?

947
00:36:01,993 --> 00:36:03,595
- Mighty.

948
00:36:03,628 --> 00:36:04,597
- You don't love him.

949
00:36:04,630 --> 00:36:06,465
You still love me,
don't you, Erica?

950
00:36:06,498 --> 00:36:08,100
- I still love you, Jake.

951
00:36:08,133 --> 00:36:09,568
Now you'd better hurry
if you want to catch Tab

952
00:36:09,602 --> 00:36:11,203
on the high road.

953
00:36:11,237 --> 00:36:14,340
I need that 15,000
to buy the Canby Judd place.

954
00:36:14,373 --> 00:36:16,008
- I'll hurry. I'll fly!

955
00:36:16,042 --> 00:36:18,010
- She thinks I'm cute.

956
00:36:19,812 --> 00:36:21,913
- Oh man, that American Hotel

957
00:36:21,947 --> 00:36:24,883
puts on a great buffet
on roadies.

958
00:36:24,917 --> 00:36:27,520
- Back in, Herman.
You're becoming a real habit.

959
00:36:27,553 --> 00:36:29,355
- Yeah, don't fool with me,
I'm a wizard.

960
00:36:29,388 --> 00:36:31,357
- There goes that outlander,
Cane Miro.

961
00:36:31,390 --> 00:36:33,325
I don't like him.
He's bound to be trouble.

962
00:36:33,358 --> 00:36:34,593
- He pushed me down.

963
00:36:36,495 --> 00:36:39,632
- Yeah, yeah, I got to pick up
a thing of wax worms.

964
00:36:39,665 --> 00:36:41,100
- I don't think he'll give us
any trouble, Josh.

965
00:36:41,133 --> 00:36:42,368
- [MUMBLING], Josh.

966
00:36:42,402 --> 00:36:45,337
- That's what Fremont said
about Stonewall Jackson.

967
00:36:45,370 --> 00:36:47,273
- Walked right into that.
- I think you're right.

968
00:36:47,307 --> 00:36:48,640
- I know I'm right.

969
00:36:48,673 --> 00:36:52,912
- Maybe I'd better find out
what his business is right now.

970
00:36:52,945 --> 00:36:54,647
- You be careful, Miss Rose.

971
00:36:54,680 --> 00:36:56,382
- He won't even know
I'm following him.

972
00:36:57,850 --> 00:36:59,685
- Hey, she's following me.

973
00:36:59,718 --> 00:37:00,787
- Oh, shoot.

974
00:37:02,855 --> 00:37:05,323
- It's going to be a bad day,
we're even out of TP.

975
00:37:09,862 --> 00:37:10,930
- A strange fellow.

976
00:37:11,997 --> 00:37:14,633
- Yeah, one more trip
to the buffet.

977
00:37:17,903 --> 00:37:19,572
- Cute outdoor cafe.

978
00:37:21,640 --> 00:37:23,775
- Yeah, she needs operatives,
all right.

979
00:37:24,943 --> 00:37:26,878
- What the -
how he gets there?

980
00:37:26,912 --> 00:37:28,080
- In a minute.

981
00:37:32,618 --> 00:37:34,587
[Hoofbeats]

982
00:37:36,955 --> 00:37:38,690
- Do I order for you, ma'am?

983
00:37:42,362 --> 00:37:45,563
- That's two guys
in a horse costume.

984
00:37:47,833 --> 00:37:49,568
- Doesn't the Marshall
have two Ls in it?

985
00:37:49,602 --> 00:37:50,637
- Well, it's either?

986
00:37:50,670 --> 00:37:52,505
- No, should have two Ls,
it looks better.

987
00:37:52,538 --> 00:37:53,772
- It's either.

988
00:37:53,805 --> 00:37:56,775
- Dennis Moore, Dennis Moore
riding through the night,

989
00:37:56,808 --> 00:37:58,643
dum, dum dum, dum,
dum, dum.

990
00:37:58,677 --> 00:38:01,380
- Hey, cameraman,
crank a little faster.

991
00:38:02,448 --> 00:38:03,848
- She does like me.

992
00:38:06,685 --> 00:38:09,655
- Oh boy, I hope there's no
black ice on their arrival.

993
00:38:11,490 --> 00:38:12,958
- Well, I think I'll stop
by the tennis court

994
00:38:12,992 --> 00:38:14,327
on the way home.

995
00:38:18,063 --> 00:38:21,800
- ♪ Marvel the Mustang
She's almost four wheel ♪

996
00:38:28,240 --> 00:38:30,977
- He likes to get
way out of town to pee.

997
00:38:32,812 --> 00:38:35,682
- What do you -
it's Jack Nicholson's place.

998
00:38:41,220 --> 00:38:43,855
- Hey, you forgot
your snack pack.

999
00:38:45,958 --> 00:38:48,327
- You knew I was
following you all the time.

1000
00:38:48,360 --> 00:38:49,428
- So did everyone else
in the county.

1001
00:38:49,462 --> 00:38:51,163
- Now that you caught me,
what do you want?

1002
00:38:51,197 --> 00:38:52,632
- You're it.

1003
00:38:52,665 --> 00:38:54,367
- Well, that's a very good
question.

1004
00:38:55,600 --> 00:38:57,937
- Why don't you get down,
take a load off your horse?

1005
00:38:57,970 --> 00:38:59,838
- Oh, and what's that
supposed to mean?

1006
00:38:59,872 --> 00:39:01,873
- How's your business
coming along?

1007
00:39:01,873 --> 00:39:04,243
- Recession hit me
like everyone else.

1008
00:39:04,277 --> 00:39:06,612
- What were you seeing
Canby Judd about?

1009
00:39:06,645 --> 00:39:09,682
- Something
that's still private.

1010
00:39:09,715 --> 00:39:12,952
- You don't have many answers
to my questions, do you?

1011
00:39:12,985 --> 00:39:15,120
- You don't ask
the right questions.

1012
00:39:15,153 --> 00:39:17,657
- Oh boy,
they're bantering again.

1013
00:39:20,225 --> 00:39:21,327
- Excuse me.

1014
00:39:21,360 --> 00:39:24,530
- When-- when you were
in Tombstone, did you...

1015
00:39:24,563 --> 00:39:25,530
- Eat much pizza?

1016
00:39:25,563 --> 00:39:27,767
- ...did you ever
run across Sam Bass?

1017
00:39:27,800 --> 00:39:29,402
- Sure. Sam and me
are old friends.

1018
00:39:29,435 --> 00:39:31,803
We used to work together.

1019
00:39:31,837 --> 00:39:33,572
- Well, he's a lawman now.

1020
00:39:33,605 --> 00:39:34,573
As a matter of fact,

1021
00:39:34,607 --> 00:39:37,777
he'll be here Saturday
to take over my job.

1022
00:39:37,810 --> 00:39:40,112
- Morning, noon, or night?

1023
00:39:40,145 --> 00:39:42,482
- [CHUCKLING]
What difference does that make?

1024
00:39:42,515 --> 00:39:44,217
- Hey, this is moving
too fast for me.

1025
00:39:44,250 --> 00:39:45,517
- Some, I guess.

1026
00:39:45,550 --> 00:39:46,518
- Hi.

1027
00:39:46,552 --> 00:39:47,753
- So, ol' Sam's
crossed over, huh?

1028
00:39:47,787 --> 00:39:48,853
- We're in dresses and stuff.

1029
00:39:48,920 --> 00:39:51,390
- Well, he'll come back.
We all do.

1030
00:39:51,423 --> 00:39:54,360
I spent a couple of months
playing Sheriff myself.

1031
00:39:54,393 --> 00:39:56,062
- How'd you make out?

1032
00:39:56,095 --> 00:39:58,363
- Plenty of action
but the pay was lousy.

1033
00:39:58,397 --> 00:39:59,465
- [Chuckling]

1034
00:39:59,498 --> 00:40:01,400
Then why is it
all the worst gunslingers

1035
00:40:01,433 --> 00:40:02,768
take up law work?

1036
00:40:02,802 --> 00:40:05,203
Look at Wyatt Earp,
Bat Masterson, Kingfisher.

1037
00:40:05,237 --> 00:40:06,205
- Yeah, good shows.

1038
00:40:06,238 --> 00:40:08,440
- Each one of them
is a cold-blooded killer.

1039
00:40:08,473 --> 00:40:10,408
- You answered that yourself.

1040
00:40:10,442 --> 00:40:13,478
For me, crime is just
a way to get rich.

1041
00:40:13,512 --> 00:40:14,680
Those other fellows
like to kill

1042
00:40:14,713 --> 00:40:17,115
and there's more chance
when it's legal,

1043
00:40:17,148 --> 00:40:19,252
like a license for murder.

1044
00:40:19,285 --> 00:40:20,953
- That is so true.

1045
00:40:20,987 --> 00:40:22,522
- Why don't you apply for one?

1046
00:40:22,555 --> 00:40:24,088
- Well, I don't have
very good references.

1047
00:40:24,123 --> 00:40:26,092
- I'll make you a deal.

1048
00:40:26,125 --> 00:40:28,593
I won't try to make you
a bad woman,

1049
00:40:28,627 --> 00:40:30,595
you stop trying to make me
a good man.

1050
00:40:30,628 --> 00:40:33,232
- It's a country song,
isn't it?

1051
00:40:33,265 --> 00:40:34,267
- You're not bad...

1052
00:40:34,300 --> 00:40:35,467
- You're just drawn that way.

1053
00:40:35,500 --> 00:40:36,735
- ...you're just no good.

1054
00:40:36,768 --> 00:40:37,970
- Is that bad?

1055
00:40:38,003 --> 00:40:40,907
- If I'm no good, might as well
take advantage of it.

1056
00:40:40,940 --> 00:40:42,742
- Sorry, my wooden hip.

1057
00:40:45,243 --> 00:40:46,212
- No thanks.

1058
00:40:46,245 --> 00:40:47,780
- I don't think [indistinct].

1059
00:40:49,215 --> 00:40:50,215
- Maybe just a little.

1060
00:40:50,248 --> 00:40:51,950
- Does this mean that they -

1061
00:40:51,983 --> 00:40:54,453
because they were going to...
and then they...

1062
00:40:58,657 --> 00:41:01,293
- Oh hold my back,
hold my back.

1063
00:41:02,293 --> 00:41:03,895
- Hi, Wormy.

1064
00:41:03,928 --> 00:41:05,998
Didn't I leave you
back at the Red Dog?

1065
00:41:06,032 --> 00:41:07,767
- Erica sent me after you.

1066
00:41:07,800 --> 00:41:09,602
She thinks
she shortchanged you.

1067
00:41:09,635 --> 00:41:11,870
- Shortchanged?
By how much?

1068
00:41:11,903 --> 00:41:13,738
- Just this.

1069
00:41:13,772 --> 00:41:15,607
- Oh, thanks.

1070
00:41:15,640 --> 00:41:17,977
Come on, honey, you're not
the Sheriff of the world.

1071
00:41:18,010 --> 00:41:19,445
- Instead of doing my job,
I'm out here

1072
00:41:19,478 --> 00:41:21,713
disgracing Scott's memory
and it's all your fault.

1073
00:41:21,747 --> 00:41:23,548
- Nothing we both do
is all my fault.

1074
00:41:23,582 --> 00:41:26,218
- Tracy and Hepburn
ran the range.

1075
00:41:26,252 --> 00:41:29,255
[♪♪♪]

1076
00:41:31,757 --> 00:41:35,227
- Wow, this is great,
kind of got a Pony Express,

1077
00:41:35,260 --> 00:41:37,930
kind of Gypsy Express thing.

1078
00:41:37,963 --> 00:41:41,067
- Whoa! Stand back,
the bullion,

1079
00:41:41,100 --> 00:41:43,568
you may be interfering
with this daring horseback rider

1080
00:41:43,602 --> 00:41:44,903
of the Pony Express,

1081
00:41:44,937 --> 00:41:47,740
carrying mail from
the Satellite of Love to the...

1082
00:41:47,773 --> 00:41:50,208
oh girl,
Satellite of Love, oh girl.

1083
00:41:50,242 --> 00:41:53,212
- Big heist man,
be gone, off with you.

1084
00:41:57,115 --> 00:42:00,285
- There goes a brave seller
in his brace horse.

1085
00:42:00,318 --> 00:42:01,387
Oh, by the way, Joel,

1086
00:42:01,420 --> 00:42:02,888
I am sending you
a very important message.

1087
00:42:02,922 --> 00:42:04,022
- Okay.

1088
00:42:04,055 --> 00:42:06,925
- I just hope to God
it gets to you in time.

1089
00:42:06,958 --> 00:42:10,795
- Servo, I'm standing
right here, what is the point?

1090
00:42:10,828 --> 00:42:12,732
- That is, Joel,
just play along.

1091
00:42:12,765 --> 00:42:16,235
- Oh, Gypsy, watch out
for that [indistinct].

1092
00:42:18,203 --> 00:42:21,440
- So, what did you write to me
in that letter?

1093
00:42:21,473 --> 00:42:23,108
- Oh, that I wanted a peanut
butter [indistinct] sandwich

1094
00:42:23,142 --> 00:42:24,843
and a Snapple for lunch.

1095
00:42:28,380 --> 00:42:30,782
- Oh, Crow, you are a lot
heavier than you appear to be.

1096
00:42:31,950 --> 00:42:34,218
- You are Joel Robinstone?

1097
00:42:34,253 --> 00:42:37,890
- That's Joel Robinson, yes,
and this is from me, I am mad.

1098
00:42:37,923 --> 00:42:39,692
Okay, let's see here.

1099
00:42:42,360 --> 00:42:43,328
"Dear Joel, how are you?

1100
00:42:43,362 --> 00:42:46,832
"I'm fine, I want
a something something

1101
00:42:46,865 --> 00:42:48,833
"and a Snapple for lunch.

1102
00:42:48,867 --> 00:42:50,435
Yours very truly, Servo."

1103
00:42:50,468 --> 00:42:52,403
Okay, I'm going to
have to respond to this.

1104
00:42:52,437 --> 00:42:53,705
- The [indistinct] sandwich,
Joel, it's very simple.

1105
00:42:53,738 --> 00:42:55,407
- Yeah, could you turn around,
let me just write this down.

1106
00:42:55,440 --> 00:42:58,977
Okay, Dear Servo,
I'm in receipt

1107
00:42:59,010 --> 00:43:00,912
of your correspondence
regarding lunch

1108
00:43:00,947 --> 00:43:03,282
and I regret to inform you
that I cannot read

1109
00:43:03,315 --> 00:43:08,053
the two words you wrote between
"I want a" and "a Snapple."

1110
00:43:08,087 --> 00:43:10,722
Please respond at your
convenience or before lunch.

1111
00:43:10,755 --> 00:43:12,323
Regards, Joel.

1112
00:43:12,357 --> 00:43:13,725
- Peanut butter
and [indistinct] sandwich,

1113
00:43:13,758 --> 00:43:15,727
it's not that difficult.

1114
00:43:15,760 --> 00:43:18,663
This is going to take forever.
I'm so hungry.

1115
00:43:22,100 --> 00:43:23,968
- Well, you know,
those kids don't even know

1116
00:43:24,002 --> 00:43:26,205
that the shortest distance
between two points

1117
00:43:26,238 --> 00:43:27,907
is a straight line.

1118
00:43:27,940 --> 00:43:30,008
- What could be
taking them so long?

1119
00:43:34,178 --> 00:43:37,282
[Buzzing]

1120
00:43:45,857 --> 00:43:47,058
- That's a lot of sandwich.

1121
00:43:49,160 --> 00:43:50,928
- Sir, you can't park here.

1122
00:43:52,798 --> 00:43:54,700
- Zebelon Tabb.

1123
00:43:54,733 --> 00:43:57,770
Third businessman to
leave Oracle this week.

1124
00:44:01,907 --> 00:44:04,543
- Hey, how did I end up back,
oh Poppy.

1125
00:44:08,180 --> 00:44:11,117
- Oh, now we're back
to June 1st.

1126
00:44:13,152 --> 00:44:16,088
- Pa, pa, I'm seeing
some trouble in town.

1127
00:44:16,122 --> 00:44:19,090
[♪♪♪]

1128
00:44:20,092 --> 00:44:21,660
- Jack Ruby on piano.

1129
00:44:21,693 --> 00:44:23,362
- Hold it.
That was lousy.

1130
00:44:23,395 --> 00:44:24,897
You've gotta get
more leg into it.

1131
00:44:24,930 --> 00:44:25,997
- Oh, why bother?

1132
00:44:26,032 --> 00:44:27,532
We've gotta be out of town
by tomorrow night.

1133
00:44:27,565 --> 00:44:29,468
- Forget it.
Go take a bath.

1134
00:44:29,502 --> 00:44:31,070
You too.
Work up a quick head.

1135
00:44:31,070 --> 00:44:34,072
- All right, I'll go change
into my leotard.

1136
00:44:35,640 --> 00:44:36,908
- Gotta talk to you.

1137
00:44:36,942 --> 00:44:37,910
- Did you get the money?

1138
00:44:37,943 --> 00:44:39,110
- Yeah, yeah.
Every penny.

1139
00:44:39,143 --> 00:44:40,278
- Hand it over.

1140
00:44:43,348 --> 00:44:44,383
- What's the matter?

1141
00:44:44,417 --> 00:44:45,883
- I killed the guy
and I wet him.

1142
00:44:45,917 --> 00:44:47,085
- Something went wrong.

1143
00:44:47,118 --> 00:44:49,722
- No, no. I didn't have time
to get rid of the body.

1144
00:44:49,755 --> 00:44:51,323
- Where is it?

1145
00:44:51,357 --> 00:44:53,525
- Cane Miro
and the lady Marshall have it.

1146
00:44:53,558 --> 00:44:54,593
- What?

1147
00:44:54,627 --> 00:44:56,062
- They're bringin' it into town.

1148
00:44:57,595 --> 00:44:59,097
- Cane and Rose?

1149
00:44:59,130 --> 00:45:00,098
- That's right.

1150
00:45:00,132 --> 00:45:02,567
- So crazy,
it just might work.

1151
00:45:02,600 --> 00:45:04,235
- Cane and Rose.

1152
00:45:04,268 --> 00:45:07,005
- This fall on
NBC [indistinct].

1153
00:45:08,273 --> 00:45:10,808
- Oh, neat, dark specter
in basic black.

1154
00:45:14,680 --> 00:45:16,482
- Rose, I've got to talk to you.

1155
00:45:16,515 --> 00:45:19,752
- It's about your yard,
the junk cars, the garbage.

1156
00:45:21,953 --> 00:45:23,555
[Knocking]

1157
00:45:23,588 --> 00:45:26,625
- Yeah, just about to give
myself a sponge bath.

1158
00:45:30,628 --> 00:45:32,998
- Doors don't open like that,
there's an--

1159
00:45:33,032 --> 00:45:34,733
he's in the hall.

1160
00:45:34,767 --> 00:45:36,067
- You crazy, coming up here?

1161
00:45:36,102 --> 00:45:37,702
- In my hallway.

1162
00:45:39,872 --> 00:45:41,107
- Well, I'll just talk to you
about it later then.

1163
00:45:41,140 --> 00:45:42,573
- Miro aims to kill Gideon.

1164
00:45:42,607 --> 00:45:44,108
I know that, honey.

1165
00:45:44,142 --> 00:45:45,110
- Where is your husband?

1166
00:45:45,143 --> 00:45:46,678
- Erica Page hid him
out in the woods.

1167
00:45:46,712 --> 00:45:48,047
- He might get ticks
or something.

1168
00:45:48,080 --> 00:45:49,247
- Erica's a busy girl.

1169
00:45:49,280 --> 00:45:51,250
- What with her volunteer
working and everything.

1170
00:45:51,283 --> 00:45:54,185
- Just what is it that Cane
has got against the Mayor?

1171
00:45:54,218 --> 00:45:55,220
- I don't know.

1172
00:45:55,253 --> 00:45:57,188
That's one story
he's never told me.

1173
00:45:57,222 --> 00:45:58,790
Now I wish he had.

1174
00:45:58,823 --> 00:45:59,892
- Well, one thing's sure.

1175
00:45:59,925 --> 00:46:01,560
He's got to be back
in town by Saturday

1176
00:46:01,593 --> 00:46:02,660
to read that letter.

1177
00:46:02,693 --> 00:46:04,328
- Yeah, he's afraid
of that, too.

1178
00:46:04,362 --> 00:46:06,998
'Fraid the town'll dry up
and blow out from under him.

1179
00:46:07,032 --> 00:46:08,500
- Like me.

1180
00:46:08,533 --> 00:46:11,203
- Erica Page is
no welfare worker.

1181
00:46:11,237 --> 00:46:13,305
Why's she taking
such an interest?

1182
00:46:13,338 --> 00:46:16,575
- Hey, she's the first woman
with her own eyebrows.

1183
00:46:16,608 --> 00:46:17,810
- Legal, of course.

1184
00:46:17,843 --> 00:46:20,612
Gideon does everything legal.

1185
00:46:20,645 --> 00:46:22,982
- Have they been involved
with Canby Judd?

1186
00:46:23,015 --> 00:46:24,415
- I believe so.

1187
00:46:24,448 --> 00:46:27,485
I know Mister Judd is thinking
of leaving Oracle.

1188
00:46:27,518 --> 00:46:29,387
- Where has Erica
hidden your husband?

1189
00:46:29,420 --> 00:46:31,790
- He's at the old Wilson Place
in Greasewood Canyon.

1190
00:46:31,823 --> 00:46:32,757
- Eugh!
- Eugh!

1191
00:46:32,790 --> 00:46:35,093
- Could you go out
and see him?

1192
00:46:35,127 --> 00:46:36,495
- I'll go first thing
in the morning.

1193
00:46:36,528 --> 00:46:38,363
- Oh, I'd appreciate that.

1194
00:46:38,397 --> 00:46:40,698
The sooner he knows you're
looking after him, the better.

1195
00:46:40,732 --> 00:46:42,533
- Hey, chocolate gorilla foot.

1196
00:46:45,137 --> 00:46:46,238
- I'd go tonight,

1197
00:46:46,272 --> 00:46:48,540
but there's something
I've got to attend to first.

1198
00:46:48,573 --> 00:46:49,542
- Thanks very much, Rose.

1199
00:46:49,575 --> 00:46:52,343
I can't tell you
how much this means to me.

1200
00:46:52,377 --> 00:46:53,312
And to think we laughed

1201
00:46:53,345 --> 00:46:55,347
at the notion of you
wearin' Scott's star.

1202
00:46:55,380 --> 00:46:56,515
Ha!

1203
00:46:56,548 --> 00:46:59,350
- Hey, a simple handshake
will do.

1204
00:46:59,383 --> 00:47:01,187
- I think she touched
my handles.

1205
00:47:03,088 --> 00:47:05,290
- Meanwhile,
back at the love shack.

1206
00:47:07,993 --> 00:47:09,595
- What time is it?

1207
00:47:09,628 --> 00:47:11,197
- You're getting careless.

1208
00:47:11,230 --> 00:47:13,165
- Yeah, sorry, I've been
on the trail a long time.

1209
00:47:13,198 --> 00:47:16,335
- Not as careless as your
little halfwit killer, Jake.

1210
00:47:16,368 --> 00:47:18,837
I recognize your subtle touch.

1211
00:47:18,870 --> 00:47:21,172
- You don't miss much, do you?

1212
00:47:21,207 --> 00:47:23,442
- You wanna watch yourself,
though.

1213
00:47:23,475 --> 00:47:26,143
Rose says there's been other
similar incidents around here.

1214
00:47:27,612 --> 00:47:29,247
- I hate it when there are
similar incidents.

1215
00:47:29,280 --> 00:47:30,482
- What was that for?

1216
00:47:30,515 --> 00:47:34,252
- So, you admit being with her
this afternoon?

1217
00:47:34,285 --> 00:47:35,387
- Sure, why not?

1218
00:47:35,420 --> 00:47:37,088
- Oh, it's fun.

1219
00:47:37,122 --> 00:47:38,190
- I've made up my mind.

1220
00:47:38,223 --> 00:47:39,457
- I'm going to be
in a poster room.

1221
00:47:39,490 --> 00:47:41,527
- I want her dead tomorrow.

1222
00:47:41,560 --> 00:47:43,095
- I told you, no changes.

1223
00:47:43,128 --> 00:47:44,362
- Now, get out of my hall.

1224
00:47:48,900 --> 00:47:50,902
- Maybe I won't
need you at all.

1225
00:47:53,738 --> 00:47:56,007
- Wait a minute, she's in
my room now, where do I go?

1226
00:48:00,712 --> 00:48:01,680
- Waiting in ambush?

1227
00:48:01,713 --> 00:48:03,048
- No, I'm wearing ambush.

1228
00:48:03,082 --> 00:48:05,717
- I don't think
I have to do that.

1229
00:48:05,750 --> 00:48:07,485
Are the girls ready to leave?

1230
00:48:07,518 --> 00:48:10,755
- You said Friday.
This is only Tuesday night.

1231
00:48:10,788 --> 00:48:12,490
- Just a reminder.

1232
00:48:12,523 --> 00:48:14,192
- Well, thank you.

1233
00:48:14,225 --> 00:48:16,795
Now that I've been reminded,
may I go on to the Red Dog?

1234
00:48:18,197 --> 00:48:20,065
- You may go to the red devil,
as far as I'm concerned.

1235
00:48:20,098 --> 00:48:23,835
- With Red Buttons
to watch Red Dwarf.

1236
00:48:23,868 --> 00:48:25,737
- This town ain't big enough
for the...

1237
00:48:25,770 --> 00:48:28,473
oh, you know the rest.

1238
00:48:28,507 --> 00:48:30,908
- Well, I feel good
after damning someone to hell.

1239
00:48:30,942 --> 00:48:32,877
I'm going to rustle me up
some Hormel Chili.

1240
00:48:34,412 --> 00:48:37,448
Say, Deputy, you shoulda seen
what I said to that Erica girl.

1241
00:48:37,482 --> 00:48:38,483
[Knocking]

1242
00:48:39,483 --> 00:48:41,453
- Come in.
- Or out.

1243
00:48:43,688 --> 00:48:45,090
- Why is all your stuff
in the hallway?

1244
00:48:45,123 --> 00:48:47,625
- Are you alone?
- Almost never, looks like.

1245
00:48:47,658 --> 00:48:48,727
- And this is hallway here.

1246
00:48:48,760 --> 00:48:49,795
- Come on in.

1247
00:48:49,828 --> 00:48:52,697
People'll think you're
collecting for the poor.

1248
00:48:52,730 --> 00:48:53,832
- In a way I guess I am.

1249
00:48:53,865 --> 00:48:56,133
- It's a walkathon.
You want to sponsor me?

1250
00:48:56,167 --> 00:48:57,168
- Sit down.

1251
00:48:59,170 --> 00:49:01,072
- Picture of yourself?

1252
00:49:01,105 --> 00:49:02,573
- Yeah, an old one.

1253
00:49:02,607 --> 00:49:04,208
- Reason I have my pants off
is...

1254
00:49:04,242 --> 00:49:06,143
- This is a Rebel officer,
isn't it?

1255
00:49:06,177 --> 00:49:07,478
- Captain Gideon Polk.

1256
00:49:07,512 --> 00:49:08,747
- Oh, the Bible guy.

1257
00:49:10,948 --> 00:49:12,683
- His wife says
you want to kill him.

1258
00:49:12,717 --> 00:49:13,852
- No, no, kiss him.

1259
00:49:13,885 --> 00:49:16,388
- I've been looking for him
since 1865 to kill him.

1260
00:49:16,422 --> 00:49:18,123
- A 100 years?
- Why?

1261
00:49:18,157 --> 00:49:19,457
- Because he lost us the war.

1262
00:49:19,490 --> 00:49:20,725
- Oh, Cane,
don't be ridiculous.

1263
00:49:20,758 --> 00:49:23,462
- As far as I'm concerned,
he did.

1264
00:49:23,495 --> 00:49:24,830
He killed my four brothers.

1265
00:49:24,863 --> 00:49:27,032
- And my forefathers.
- What?

1266
00:49:27,032 --> 00:49:28,600
- Oh, he didn't murder them
outright.

1267
00:49:28,633 --> 00:49:30,535
He just fed them
to the Yankees.

1268
00:49:30,568 --> 00:49:32,503
- With a choice of salad,
soup, or juice.

1269
00:49:32,537 --> 00:49:34,505
- What happened?

1270
00:49:34,538 --> 00:49:36,875
- Like the man says,
it's a long story.

1271
00:49:36,908 --> 00:49:38,743
- Do you have any sisters?

1272
00:49:38,777 --> 00:49:39,978
- I've got nothing to do.

1273
00:49:42,747 --> 00:49:44,683
- Are you familiar with
the Battle of Lookout Mountain?

1274
00:49:44,717 --> 00:49:45,983
- That's one of
my favorite battles.

1275
00:49:46,017 --> 00:49:47,118
- Only what I learned
in school.

1276
00:49:49,855 --> 00:49:51,322
- Oh boy, here we go.

1277
00:49:51,357 --> 00:49:53,125
- My brothers and I
were in an artillery battery

1278
00:49:53,158 --> 00:49:55,760
commanded by Captain Polk.

1279
00:49:55,793 --> 00:49:58,063
Lookout Mountain was where
we had the Yankees under siege.

1280
00:49:58,097 --> 00:49:59,430
- With Steven Seagal.

1281
00:49:59,463 --> 00:50:00,832
- They had to get us
off those ridges

1282
00:50:00,865 --> 00:50:02,167
but they couldn't.

1283
00:50:02,200 --> 00:50:03,435
- But they did.
- But they couldn't.

1284
00:50:03,468 --> 00:50:04,970
- Our position
was impregnable.

1285
00:50:05,003 --> 00:50:06,103
- You can't say that.

1286
00:50:06,137 --> 00:50:08,373
- The only weak line was
at the foot of the ridge.

1287
00:50:08,407 --> 00:50:09,607
- Sing out, brother.

1288
00:50:09,640 --> 00:50:11,877
- Polk's battery was in a
beautiful position halfway down.

1289
00:50:11,910 --> 00:50:13,245
- Righteous.

1290
00:50:13,278 --> 00:50:14,813
- The Yankees charged.

1291
00:50:14,847 --> 00:50:16,180
Grant ordered them to take
only the trench

1292
00:50:16,213 --> 00:50:17,182
at the edge of the ridge.

1293
00:50:17,215 --> 00:50:18,183
- Testify.

1294
00:50:18,217 --> 00:50:20,585
- We had 'em cold
and we cut 'em down,

1295
00:50:20,618 --> 00:50:21,553
but they massed again

1296
00:50:21,587 --> 00:50:22,953
and came out again
over their own dead.

1297
00:50:22,987 --> 00:50:24,155
- Take it home.

1298
00:50:24,188 --> 00:50:25,957
- A shell exploded inside
the body of a Yankee officer

1299
00:50:25,990 --> 00:50:27,125
right in front of us.

1300
00:50:27,158 --> 00:50:28,093
- I had to ask.

1301
00:50:28,127 --> 00:50:30,228
- Polk saw it and ran.

1302
00:50:30,262 --> 00:50:33,298
At the height of our victory,
he ran.

1303
00:50:33,332 --> 00:50:34,900
My brothers and a few of us
stuck with the guns

1304
00:50:34,933 --> 00:50:38,070
but the fever caught
and the whole center broke.

1305
00:50:38,103 --> 00:50:39,203
It was the end of the battle

1306
00:50:39,237 --> 00:50:42,773
and that meant
the end of the war.

1307
00:50:42,807 --> 00:50:44,977
- I'm just hitting
the highlights here.

1308
00:50:45,010 --> 00:50:49,013
- My brothers were killed
and I was captured.

1309
00:50:49,047 --> 00:50:51,082
- Live and learn.
- I don't quite understand.

1310
00:50:51,115 --> 00:50:53,352
- Who won?
- It's very simple.

1311
00:50:53,385 --> 00:50:54,485
If we hadn't lost
Lookout Mountain,

1312
00:50:54,518 --> 00:50:56,622
there would've been
an armistice.

1313
00:50:56,655 --> 00:50:58,190
The Confederacy
would still be alive.

1314
00:50:58,223 --> 00:50:59,557
- And the Vikings would have
finally captured

1315
00:50:59,590 --> 00:51:01,860
a Superbowl title.

1316
00:51:01,893 --> 00:51:04,963
- One man,
one stinking coward.

1317
00:51:04,997 --> 00:51:07,898
- You're not buying this,
are you?

1318
00:51:07,932 --> 00:51:08,900
- I'm sorry.

1319
00:51:08,933 --> 00:51:10,502
- Well, at least
you had brothers.

1320
00:51:10,535 --> 00:51:12,670
- Guess I was beginning to
live it all over again.

1321
00:51:15,340 --> 00:51:16,307
- Don't apologize.

1322
00:51:16,340 --> 00:51:17,608
- I'm sorry.

1323
00:51:17,642 --> 00:51:19,243
- I know how you feel about it.

1324
00:51:19,277 --> 00:51:20,845
- Do you have any snacks
or anything?

1325
00:51:20,878 --> 00:51:24,248
- But you're wrong.
You're dead wrong.

1326
00:51:24,282 --> 00:51:25,950
You can't blame
the loss of a war

1327
00:51:25,983 --> 00:51:27,652
on one low-ranking officer.

1328
00:51:27,685 --> 00:51:28,920
- I can't?

1329
00:51:28,953 --> 00:51:30,588
- You weren't there.

1330
00:51:30,622 --> 00:51:32,823
- That doesn't make
any difference.

1331
00:51:32,857 --> 00:51:34,192
Don't you think
the remembrance of this

1332
00:51:34,225 --> 00:51:37,062
has made him pay for it
over and over again?

1333
00:51:37,095 --> 00:51:38,863
- Million men
died for nothing.

1334
00:51:38,897 --> 00:51:40,665
- Only one poor
Green Berets.

1335
00:51:40,698 --> 00:51:43,435
- But as I said before,
they all die sooner or later.

1336
00:51:43,468 --> 00:51:44,468
- When did he say that?

1337
00:51:44,502 --> 00:51:46,170
- And sooner or later,
everyone runs...

1338
00:51:46,203 --> 00:51:47,705
- Especially Wilma Rudolf.

1339
00:51:47,738 --> 00:51:49,407
- ...at least once.

1340
00:51:49,440 --> 00:51:51,042
- Forget it!

1341
00:51:51,075 --> 00:51:52,610
- Well, our hour's
almost up here.

1342
00:51:52,643 --> 00:51:54,478
Did you want to
schedule next week?

1343
00:51:57,015 --> 00:51:58,917
- You'll leave him alone?

1344
00:51:58,950 --> 00:52:00,818
- Sure.
- I'm not that mad.

1345
00:52:00,852 --> 00:52:04,122
Easier than Dead in the West.

1346
00:52:04,155 --> 00:52:06,057
- Now, she's in my room again.

1347
00:52:06,090 --> 00:52:07,492
What am I gonna do?

1348
00:52:08,960 --> 00:52:11,597
- Hold it. Who is it?

1349
00:52:11,630 --> 00:52:12,963
- Beauregard and Sapp.

1350
00:52:12,997 --> 00:52:14,933
- They're cops.

1351
00:52:14,967 --> 00:52:17,202
- Sorry, Rose.

1352
00:52:17,235 --> 00:52:18,437
I'm getting' a little jumpy.

1353
00:52:18,470 --> 00:52:20,038
- Which one's Beauregard?
- Felicity send you?

1354
00:52:20,072 --> 00:52:22,140
- How about some crystal light,
just made a patch.

1355
00:52:22,173 --> 00:52:23,342
- She told me you were here.

1356
00:52:23,375 --> 00:52:25,677
I just came to tell you
you were safe.

1357
00:52:25,710 --> 00:52:26,978
- You mean, Cane Miro's gone?

1358
00:52:27,012 --> 00:52:29,047
- Well, no, he's not gone,
but I gave him until Saturday

1359
00:52:29,080 --> 00:52:31,048
to finish his business and ride.

1360
00:52:31,082 --> 00:52:33,918
- Oh, so that's what
Erica meant.

1361
00:52:33,952 --> 00:52:36,220
- They're stuck in a sub-plot.
- Erica?

1362
00:52:36,253 --> 00:52:37,355
- Yeah, she said he'd be gone

1363
00:52:37,388 --> 00:52:39,858
after that railroad letter
arrived.

1364
00:52:39,892 --> 00:52:41,827
- What's Erica got to do
with Cane?

1365
00:52:41,860 --> 00:52:43,362
- She didn't say,

1366
00:52:43,395 --> 00:52:45,997
but it can't be for a date
for Sunday school.

1367
00:52:47,665 --> 00:52:49,533
- I have to go over here
and think about this.

1368
00:52:49,567 --> 00:52:51,770
This one is deep.

1369
00:52:51,803 --> 00:52:54,672
- Everything seems to add up
to Saturday:

1370
00:52:54,705 --> 00:52:58,142
Railroad letter, Sam Bass,
my ultimatum to Cane...

1371
00:52:58,175 --> 00:52:59,277
- The fishing opener.

1372
00:52:59,310 --> 00:53:02,213
- ...now Erica.

1373
00:53:02,247 --> 00:53:03,482
Well, if she's not careful,

1374
00:53:03,515 --> 00:53:05,217
she's gonna connive herself
right into one of my cells.

1375
00:53:05,250 --> 00:53:06,818
- Careful,
that's the main beam there.

1376
00:53:06,852 --> 00:53:08,320
- Well, I just want to be sure
you're comfortable.

1377
00:53:08,353 --> 00:53:09,287
Have you got
everything you need?

1378
00:53:09,320 --> 00:53:11,723
- Eh, I could use
a few troops of cavalry.

1379
00:53:11,757 --> 00:53:12,757
- I don't get it.

1380
00:53:12,790 --> 00:53:14,158
- For now, Josh and I
will have to do.

1381
00:53:14,192 --> 00:53:16,027
I'll tell Felicity
that you're here.

1382
00:53:16,060 --> 00:53:18,597
- Are you sure
he can't find me here?

1383
00:53:18,630 --> 00:53:20,565
- He'd never find you
in a million years.

1384
00:53:20,598 --> 00:53:23,768
- Apparently they haven't
invented peripheral vision.

1385
00:53:23,802 --> 00:53:25,170
- Well, goodbye, Rose.

1386
00:53:25,203 --> 00:53:26,605
- I'll see you on Saturday.

1387
00:53:26,638 --> 00:53:27,772
Come, boy.

1388
00:53:27,805 --> 00:53:30,442
- Guess the Beverly girl
can stay as the Beverly girl

1389
00:53:30,475 --> 00:53:31,475
in the hotel.

1390
00:53:32,710 --> 00:53:34,645
- I like that Sheriff, I do.

1391
00:53:36,180 --> 00:53:39,952
- Don't sleep
in the subway, oh hi.

1392
00:53:39,985 --> 00:53:42,087
- Where did you
just come from?

1393
00:53:42,120 --> 00:53:43,288
- If I wanted to kill Polk,

1394
00:53:43,322 --> 00:53:45,323
I wouldn't be out here
waiting to talk to you about it.

1395
00:53:45,357 --> 00:53:47,225
- Don't try and paint
yourself white.

1396
00:53:47,258 --> 00:53:48,660
- I'm not.

1397
00:53:48,693 --> 00:53:50,295
I'm the same man
that's wanted in three states

1398
00:53:50,328 --> 00:53:51,930
and five territories,
remember.

1399
00:53:51,963 --> 00:53:53,498
- And I'm not allowed
in the sizzler.

1400
00:53:53,532 --> 00:53:55,467
- Tell me, am I wanted
around here?

1401
00:53:55,500 --> 00:53:57,302
- Don't start.

1402
00:53:57,335 --> 00:53:59,237
- Not yet.

1403
00:53:59,270 --> 00:54:01,072
- I've only got
three more days, huh?

1404
00:54:01,105 --> 00:54:02,707
- So have I.

1405
00:54:02,740 --> 00:54:04,208
- What do you mean by that?

1406
00:54:04,242 --> 00:54:06,745
- Until Sam Bass
comes to take over.

1407
00:54:06,778 --> 00:54:08,178
- Ah.

1408
00:54:08,212 --> 00:54:10,115
- Man, look what's coming
out of your horse?

1409
00:54:10,148 --> 00:54:11,950
- Look, let's go back
to town together.

1410
00:54:16,287 --> 00:54:19,090
- Welcome to Rice Lake Key Club
music by Tangerine.

1411
00:54:19,123 --> 00:54:20,092
- See you around.

1412
00:54:20,125 --> 00:54:22,260
- Later.
I have some special business.

1413
00:54:22,293 --> 00:54:23,662
- What kind of business?

1414
00:54:23,695 --> 00:54:26,063
- I told you before,
it's private.

1415
00:54:26,097 --> 00:54:27,198
But I tell you what,

1416
00:54:27,232 --> 00:54:28,733
meet me just outside of town
where the road forks

1417
00:54:28,767 --> 00:54:30,368
and I'll tell you
all about it.

1418
00:54:30,402 --> 00:54:31,435
- That a promise?

1419
00:54:31,468 --> 00:54:33,772
- Make it tomorrow night
at eight o'clock.

1420
00:54:33,805 --> 00:54:34,805
- Can I hear this?

1421
00:54:34,838 --> 00:54:35,840
- Why not today?

1422
00:54:35,873 --> 00:54:38,208
- I'm busy.
Tomorrow at eight.

1423
00:54:38,242 --> 00:54:40,645
- Are you picking me up?
What should I wear?

1424
00:54:40,678 --> 00:54:41,913
- Joshua,
climb to your horse.

1425
00:54:41,947 --> 00:54:42,880
Hurry up.

1426
00:54:42,913 --> 00:54:43,882
- What's goin' on?

1427
00:54:43,915 --> 00:54:45,317
- Quick!
We haven't a minute to lose.

1428
00:54:45,350 --> 00:54:48,018
- Okay, give me a minute.
I just woke up.

1429
00:54:48,052 --> 00:54:51,422
[♪♪♪]

1430
00:54:51,455 --> 00:54:53,992
- So she followed him
and he followed her,

1431
00:54:54,025 --> 00:54:55,727
and now she's following him
following her?

1432
00:54:55,760 --> 00:54:56,728
- Yeah.

1433
00:54:58,997 --> 00:55:01,767
- Looks like the terror
of tiny town got bigger.

1434
00:55:03,968 --> 00:55:04,935
- Call the office
and tell them

1435
00:55:04,968 --> 00:55:07,138
to fax a pair of handcuffs
to us.

1436
00:55:17,482 --> 00:55:20,018
- Careful, traffic from left
does not stop.

1437
00:55:22,920 --> 00:55:24,155
- What you're thinking about?

1438
00:55:26,057 --> 00:55:28,058
- Easy snap ranch
ready to assemble.

1439
00:55:28,092 --> 00:55:29,593
- On your horse, Mayor!

1440
00:55:29,627 --> 00:55:31,495
- What is it?

1441
00:55:31,528 --> 00:55:33,063
- Cane Miro.

1442
00:55:33,097 --> 00:55:34,098
Hurry up.

1443
00:55:36,902 --> 00:55:38,235
Faster!
- Don't watch.

1444
00:55:38,268 --> 00:55:39,270
I can't do it
when you're watching.

1445
00:55:41,305 --> 00:55:42,840
- Come on, come on!
Let's go.

1446
00:55:42,873 --> 00:55:44,375
- I'm doing it.

1447
00:55:44,408 --> 00:55:46,210
- Come on, boys.

1448
00:55:46,243 --> 00:55:47,278
- What was that thing there?

1449
00:55:47,312 --> 00:55:49,947
- Whatever it is,
it wasn't invented yet.

1450
00:55:49,980 --> 00:55:52,650
[♪♪♪]

1451
00:55:52,683 --> 00:55:55,487
- This greasewood canyon's
real pretty.

1452
00:55:55,520 --> 00:55:57,188
I was picturing a lot of
grease and stuff,

1453
00:55:57,222 --> 00:55:59,923
no grease at all,
just like anywhere else.

1454
00:56:02,493 --> 00:56:04,262
- He was hiding out
at Wall Drug.

1455
00:56:06,063 --> 00:56:08,065
- Pretty nice, like the cedar.

1456
00:56:08,098 --> 00:56:10,335
Done a lot of work here,
added a sunroom back here.

1457
00:56:13,572 --> 00:56:14,705
- I can't wear black.

1458
00:56:19,143 --> 00:56:22,147
- Oh what a mess,
there's garbage everywhere.

1459
00:56:22,180 --> 00:56:24,282
- Need any help, boss?

1460
00:56:26,050 --> 00:56:28,085
- Dear Steve,
stopped by, you weren't here,

1461
00:56:28,118 --> 00:56:29,687
borrowed some pants
and a blouse,

1462
00:56:29,720 --> 00:56:31,857
hope you don't mind.

1463
00:56:31,890 --> 00:56:33,157
Keith.

1464
00:56:33,190 --> 00:56:35,327
- Alright, Herman,
back to sleep.

1465
00:56:35,360 --> 00:56:38,597
- Sheriff took me
walking today.

1466
00:56:38,630 --> 00:56:41,498
- Forget it.
- What'd he do?

1467
00:56:41,532 --> 00:56:43,468
- How's it going?
- Calling it leaves of grass.

1468
00:56:43,502 --> 00:56:47,772
- Well, it's not Windsor Castle,
but if it'll keep a killer in,

1469
00:56:47,805 --> 00:56:49,807
it'll keep a killer out.

1470
00:56:49,840 --> 00:56:51,042
- I could shoot you.

1471
00:56:53,445 --> 00:56:54,645
I hope this isn't
a conjugal visit.

1472
00:56:54,678 --> 00:56:55,680
- Hello, Felicity.
- Gideon!

1473
00:56:55,713 --> 00:56:57,615
- Boy, this is better
than Fanta Suites.

1474
00:56:57,648 --> 00:56:59,417
- I'm gonna take the cure.

1475
00:56:59,450 --> 00:57:00,752
- What, Rogaine?

1476
00:57:02,287 --> 00:57:04,255
[♪♪♪]

1477
00:57:04,288 --> 00:57:07,092
- Man, this week
has really dragged on.

1478
00:57:10,328 --> 00:57:12,730
- Oh, I just couldn't, with
the drunk looking right at us.

1479
00:57:12,763 --> 00:57:14,965
- I wish Rose would get back.

1480
00:57:14,998 --> 00:57:15,700
I don't like it
when she's away.

1481
00:57:16,802 --> 00:57:19,237
- Don't worry, Mayor Polk.
I'll talk care of you.

1482
00:57:19,270 --> 00:57:21,172
- Where are your cannons,
Captain?

1483
00:57:21,205 --> 00:57:22,407
- That's far enough, Mister.
You get out of here.

1484
00:57:22,440 --> 00:57:24,075
- I'll get out of here
when I'm ready.

1485
00:57:24,075 --> 00:57:25,910
Look out, Captain.
Yankees comin'.

1486
00:57:25,943 --> 00:57:26,945
- I said out.

1487
00:57:29,047 --> 00:57:30,515
- All right,
I was just leaving.

1488
00:57:36,353 --> 00:57:38,388
- I like ladies,
just not of dancer.

1489
00:57:42,860 --> 00:57:44,695
- Man, this movie
is just sitting on my head

1490
00:57:44,728 --> 00:57:45,897
and crushing it.

1491
00:57:47,798 --> 00:57:50,568
- Oh, right in my vast
doughy mid-section.

1492
00:57:50,602 --> 00:57:53,437
[All whooping]

1493
00:57:56,107 --> 00:57:58,543
- Not exactly
the riot about it, fellas.

1494
00:57:58,577 --> 00:58:01,212
[Grunting]

1495
00:58:01,245 --> 00:58:03,280
- Whoa, watch
the bumper pool table!

1496
00:58:09,353 --> 00:58:10,355
- Well...

1497
00:58:11,322 --> 00:58:12,723
There goes the façade.

1498
00:58:21,832 --> 00:58:23,433
- And now it's a hat
made out of an otter.

1499
00:58:26,037 --> 00:58:27,405
- Well done, [indistinct].

1500
00:58:27,438 --> 00:58:28,405
- What happened here?

1501
00:58:28,438 --> 00:58:30,140
- He tried to kill me.

1502
00:58:30,173 --> 00:58:31,642
- I wouldn't spit on him.

1503
00:58:31,675 --> 00:58:33,043
- Joshua saved my life.

1504
00:58:33,077 --> 00:58:34,712
- We will return to
non-sequiturial theater.

1505
00:58:34,745 --> 00:58:38,015
- Alright, clear out.
We'll worry about this later.

1506
00:58:38,048 --> 00:58:38,983
- I'll see you later.

1507
00:58:39,017 --> 00:58:40,652
- I'll think about it.

1508
00:58:40,685 --> 00:58:42,620
- We're still on
for 8:00 though, aren't we?

1509
00:58:44,755 --> 00:58:46,892
- Good one, Brad.

1510
00:58:46,925 --> 00:58:48,192
- I'm alright, Miss Rose.

1511
00:58:48,225 --> 00:58:50,795
- Next time, Joshua,
if you have to, use a gun.

1512
00:58:50,828 --> 00:58:52,263
- I didn't give him a chance
to try anything.

1513
00:58:52,297 --> 00:58:54,265
- I'm not that kind of girl.

1514
00:58:54,298 --> 00:58:55,767
- I'm going over
to the Red Dog.

1515
00:58:55,800 --> 00:58:57,435
- They're hiring dancers.

1516
00:58:57,468 --> 00:58:59,003
- You big galoot.

1517
00:59:00,805 --> 00:59:02,207
- Tilly upstairs?

1518
00:59:02,240 --> 00:59:03,207
- She's tripping.

1519
00:59:03,240 --> 00:59:04,775
- That's a switch.

1520
00:59:04,808 --> 00:59:05,943
- Well, I'm going to pay
you girls off today

1521
00:59:05,977 --> 00:59:08,647
so you can get
an early start tomorrow.

1522
00:59:08,680 --> 00:59:10,782
- I sure hate to go.

1523
00:59:10,815 --> 00:59:11,782
- I hate to lose you.

1524
00:59:11,815 --> 00:59:13,550
- It's been so fun
with you and everybody.

1525
00:59:13,585 --> 00:59:15,687
- ...rough time
finding three more girls.

1526
00:59:15,720 --> 00:59:17,322
- Poor Erica.

1527
00:59:17,355 --> 00:59:18,355
- Why don't you
get out of those pants

1528
00:59:18,388 --> 00:59:19,890
and find yourself a man?

1529
00:59:19,923 --> 00:59:21,525
- I'm wearing these pants
because I lost a man,

1530
00:59:21,558 --> 00:59:23,427
a better man than ever
slipped you a dollar.

1531
00:59:23,460 --> 00:59:24,428
- Ouch.

1532
00:59:24,462 --> 00:59:26,363
- There's no law
against tipping.

1533
00:59:26,397 --> 00:59:28,132
- Sundown tomorrow?

1534
00:59:28,165 --> 00:59:29,967
- They're leaving
in the morning.

1535
00:59:30,000 --> 00:59:32,437
- I just wanted to make sure
everything was clear.

1536
00:59:32,470 --> 00:59:33,805
Bye, girls.

1537
00:59:33,838 --> 00:59:35,473
- Bye.
- Bye.

1538
00:59:35,507 --> 00:59:38,175
- What's she got
against us anyway?

1539
00:59:38,208 --> 00:59:40,345
- She thinks you violated
the sanitary code.

1540
00:59:40,378 --> 00:59:41,612
- That's my job.

1541
00:59:41,645 --> 00:59:44,482
- I could kill that woman.

1542
00:59:44,515 --> 00:59:46,217
- Why, Tessie,
that's against the law.

1543
00:59:46,250 --> 00:59:48,518
- I still want to.

1544
00:59:48,552 --> 00:59:52,790
- 'Course, it could be
a coincidental accident.

1545
00:59:52,823 --> 00:59:53,792
With dear Rose gone,

1546
00:59:53,825 --> 00:59:55,025
you'd have no reason
to leave town.

1547
00:59:55,058 --> 00:59:57,795
- She's paying them
in television warranties.

1548
00:59:57,828 --> 00:59:59,030
- Girls, you've been
so good for business

1549
00:59:59,063 --> 01:00:01,065
I'm going to give you
a little going-away bonus.

1550
01:00:01,098 --> 01:00:02,533
- Wampum bucks.

1551
01:00:10,575 --> 01:00:13,645
- Well, I'll see if I can
borrow a dollar from Ed.

1552
01:00:16,280 --> 01:00:19,217
- Hello, there.
- I thought you were Dale.

1553
01:00:19,250 --> 01:00:21,718
- What are you doing up there?

1554
01:00:21,752 --> 01:00:24,622
- On the lookout
for gorgeous marshals.

1555
01:00:24,655 --> 01:00:26,157
- You didn't need to
scare me half to death.

1556
01:00:26,190 --> 01:00:27,925
- Come on up.

1557
01:00:27,958 --> 01:00:29,527
- Don't be silly.

1558
01:00:29,560 --> 01:00:31,128
- You wanna learn
my business, don't ya?

1559
01:00:31,162 --> 01:00:32,230
- You did your business
up there?

1560
01:00:32,263 --> 01:00:33,965
- I almost didn't come at all.

1561
01:00:33,998 --> 01:00:36,867
- I almost didn't expect you,
but now that you're here,

1562
01:00:36,900 --> 01:00:38,068
if you want to find out
anything,

1563
01:00:38,102 --> 01:00:39,537
you'll have to come up
in this tree.

1564
01:00:39,570 --> 01:00:41,372
- I made a really cool
fort up here.

1565
01:00:41,405 --> 01:00:43,007
- Twelve-year-old!

1566
01:00:43,040 --> 01:00:45,275
- I spent that much time
in prison.

1567
01:00:45,308 --> 01:00:47,312
- Alright, little boy,
I'll come up.

1568
01:00:47,345 --> 01:00:49,413
- Gee, you're not as small
as you look.

1569
01:00:49,447 --> 01:00:50,482
[Panting]

1570
01:00:50,515 --> 01:00:52,783
- You can make me
feel young again.

1571
01:00:52,817 --> 01:00:55,152
- Alright, start talking.

1572
01:00:55,185 --> 01:00:57,855
[Crow babbling]

1573
01:00:57,888 --> 01:01:00,358
- Look, you can see
the Big Dipper.

1574
01:01:00,392 --> 01:01:01,558
- Hey, it's daytime.

1575
01:01:01,592 --> 01:01:02,593
- He's not talking
about the stars.

1576
01:01:02,627 --> 01:01:03,828
- I don't know.

1577
01:01:03,862 --> 01:01:05,997
- Now come down
from the stars.

1578
01:01:06,030 --> 01:01:07,098
- Okay.

1579
01:01:07,132 --> 01:01:10,535
- I want to know exactly
what you're after here.

1580
01:01:10,568 --> 01:01:11,702
- Very little.

1581
01:01:11,735 --> 01:01:13,705
- Just this.

1582
01:01:21,245 --> 01:01:22,413
- I know all about that.

1583
01:01:22,447 --> 01:01:24,882
- We had a class
in fifth grade.

1584
01:01:24,915 --> 01:01:26,950
- I mean, other things.

1585
01:01:26,983 --> 01:01:29,453
- What other things?

1586
01:01:29,487 --> 01:01:31,588
- Oh.
- Tasty frees.

1587
01:01:31,622 --> 01:01:34,725
- ...things about Oracle.

1588
01:01:34,758 --> 01:01:35,860
- Nice place.

1589
01:01:35,893 --> 01:01:37,828
- Hear they got a meat packing
plant there.

1590
01:01:39,797 --> 01:01:42,100
- You tried to kill Polk today.

1591
01:01:42,133 --> 01:01:43,100
- I love you.

1592
01:01:43,133 --> 01:01:44,302
- I'm glad you didn't
get to him.

1593
01:01:44,335 --> 01:01:45,570
I'd have had to kill you.

1594
01:01:45,603 --> 01:01:49,440
- Now he's the sensitive
cold-blooded killer.

1595
01:01:49,473 --> 01:01:51,342
- But you didn't.

1596
01:01:51,375 --> 01:01:52,443
- I love you.

1597
01:01:53,845 --> 01:01:55,413
- I thought
that's what you said.

1598
01:01:57,548 --> 01:01:58,917
- I'm glad you're here.

1599
01:01:58,950 --> 01:02:01,385
- So how's this going to affect
his contract to kill her?

1600
01:02:05,122 --> 01:02:07,692
- Tastiest sheriff
in the old west.

1601
01:02:07,725 --> 01:02:09,427
- I wish that...

1602
01:02:09,460 --> 01:02:11,162
- What?
- You were somebody else.

1603
01:02:11,195 --> 01:02:14,132
- ...[SIGHS] I have to
throw you out and I...

1604
01:02:14,165 --> 01:02:15,467
- Motel matches.

1605
01:02:15,500 --> 01:02:17,602
- ...I wish I didn't have to.

1606
01:02:17,635 --> 01:02:20,572
- No one's
making you do anything.

1607
01:02:20,605 --> 01:02:21,872
- I won't change my mind...

1608
01:02:21,905 --> 01:02:24,042
- Well, maybe I will.
No, no I won't.

1609
01:02:24,075 --> 01:02:26,710
- ...but I can make-believe
a little.

1610
01:02:26,743 --> 01:02:28,612
- Sure you can.

1611
01:02:28,645 --> 01:02:29,613
We both can.

1612
01:02:29,647 --> 01:02:31,682
- Let's make belief
I have talent.

1613
01:02:31,715 --> 01:02:33,183
- Did you tell anyone
I ordered you out of town

1614
01:02:33,217 --> 01:02:34,985
by Saturday?

1615
01:02:35,018 --> 01:02:36,953
- Not a soul.

1616
01:02:36,987 --> 01:02:39,457
- Well, pretend
I'm taking it all back.

1617
01:02:39,490 --> 01:02:41,325
You stay here forever
and I'll...

1618
01:02:41,358 --> 01:02:43,127
- Forget it.

1619
01:02:45,228 --> 01:02:47,132
- You didn't tell anyone?

1620
01:02:47,165 --> 01:02:49,967
- That's what I said.

1621
01:02:50,000 --> 01:02:51,535
- Except Erica.

1622
01:02:51,568 --> 01:02:52,870
- Erica, last of all.

1623
01:02:52,903 --> 01:02:55,840
- She was the last person
I didn't tell.

1624
01:02:55,873 --> 01:02:57,275
- You're lying to me.

1625
01:02:57,308 --> 01:02:58,275
- I wouldn't lie to you.

1626
01:02:58,308 --> 01:03:00,077
- She knows.
She told Polk.

1627
01:03:00,110 --> 01:03:03,815
- Okay, I would lie to you.
- So she knows.

1628
01:03:03,848 --> 01:03:05,015
We're making believe,
aren't we?

1629
01:03:05,048 --> 01:03:07,385
- That isn't why you lied.

1630
01:03:07,418 --> 01:03:08,953
How does Erica fit
into your plans?

1631
01:03:08,987 --> 01:03:11,155
- What is this?
Come on, what's love?

1632
01:03:11,188 --> 01:03:12,390
- Is she in this tree?

1633
01:03:15,425 --> 01:03:17,728
- I don't know.

1634
01:03:17,762 --> 01:03:19,963
Is she?

1635
01:03:19,997 --> 01:03:21,165
- Don't be a fool.

1636
01:03:21,198 --> 01:03:23,868
- [indistinct]
what's that, baby?

1637
01:03:23,902 --> 01:03:26,903
It means, may I borrow
your bicycle.

1638
01:03:26,937 --> 01:03:29,207
- I can't trust you.

1639
01:03:29,240 --> 01:03:31,375
- I know you can't.

1640
01:03:31,408 --> 01:03:32,610
I'd laugh at anyone that did...

1641
01:03:32,643 --> 01:03:34,145
- Ha-ha.

1642
01:03:34,178 --> 01:03:36,047
- ...but I swear to you
I was telling God's truth

1643
01:03:36,080 --> 01:03:37,748
when I said I loved you.

1644
01:03:37,782 --> 01:03:40,752
- Maybe you were, Cane.

1645
01:03:40,785 --> 01:03:44,088
Maybe it's a two-way thing.

1646
01:03:44,122 --> 01:03:46,057
- With the center
passing lane.

1647
01:03:46,090 --> 01:03:47,325
- But there's one thing
I mustn't forget...

1648
01:03:47,358 --> 01:03:48,492
- Jello.

1649
01:03:48,525 --> 01:03:50,762
- ...and that's that we're
sworn enemies, you and I.

1650
01:03:50,795 --> 01:03:52,863
All the moonlight in heaven
can't change that.

1651
01:03:52,897 --> 01:03:54,298
- Oh yeah, that's right.

1652
01:03:56,267 --> 01:03:58,135
- It's too late
to create conflict.

1653
01:03:58,168 --> 01:03:59,170
- Goodnight, Cane.

1654
01:04:00,270 --> 01:04:02,473
- She is one spooky chick.

1655
01:04:02,507 --> 01:04:05,342
[Hoofbeats]

1656
01:04:08,678 --> 01:04:10,013
- My enemies don't realize

1657
01:04:10,047 --> 01:04:13,083
that I'm very vulnerable
right now.

1658
01:04:15,687 --> 01:04:18,388
- What is the crew
doing in this shot?

1659
01:04:18,422 --> 01:04:19,957
- In this movie,
you can just pick any object

1660
01:04:19,990 --> 01:04:21,158
and hide behind it.

1661
01:04:21,192 --> 01:04:22,227
- Let go of me!

1662
01:04:22,260 --> 01:04:23,995
- All right, what are these?

1663
01:04:24,028 --> 01:04:26,597
- Looks like they've all been
to Flea Farm.

1664
01:04:28,698 --> 01:04:29,667
- What are you doing?

1665
01:04:29,700 --> 01:04:31,102
- This is for taking
my loop lighter.

1666
01:04:31,135 --> 01:04:33,637
- She's been attacked
by babes in Toyland.

1667
01:04:33,670 --> 01:04:34,605
- And the [indistinct] here.

1668
01:04:34,638 --> 01:04:36,173
- Oh yes.

1669
01:04:36,207 --> 01:04:38,542
- You're going to watch us dance
if it's the last thing you do.

1670
01:04:41,612 --> 01:04:43,547
- Oh come on, you know
I don't wear necklaces.

1671
01:04:43,580 --> 01:04:44,815
- Stop it! Let go!

1672
01:04:44,848 --> 01:04:46,450
- This isn't funny.

1673
01:04:46,483 --> 01:04:48,452
[♪♪♪]

1674
01:04:48,485 --> 01:04:50,120
- Don't let 'em get away!

1675
01:04:50,153 --> 01:04:52,088
[Gunshot]

1676
01:04:52,122 --> 01:04:53,857
Don't bother
going back to town.

1677
01:04:53,890 --> 01:04:56,027
I want you out
of this state tonight.

1678
01:04:56,060 --> 01:04:57,295
- What about our clothes?

1679
01:04:57,328 --> 01:04:59,063
- Now!

1680
01:04:59,097 --> 01:05:00,230
If you ever come back
to Oracle again,

1681
01:05:00,263 --> 01:05:01,332
I'll kill all three of you.

1682
01:05:01,365 --> 01:05:03,033
- I'm just so upset right now.

1683
01:05:03,067 --> 01:05:05,268
- Move!

1684
01:05:05,302 --> 01:05:06,703
- This bites.
Let's go grattle.

1685
01:05:06,737 --> 01:05:07,805
- Told you, it wouldn't work.

1686
01:05:07,838 --> 01:05:09,540
- It wasn't my idea, Cindy.

1687
01:05:09,573 --> 01:05:11,242
- My new shirt.

1688
01:05:11,275 --> 01:05:12,577
[Hoofbeats]

1689
01:05:12,610 --> 01:05:14,412
- She didn't even cry
when her husband died.

1690
01:05:17,047 --> 01:05:19,817
- The British were coming,
the British were coming.

1691
01:05:20,818 --> 01:05:22,085
- Little man, my man.

1692
01:05:24,788 --> 01:05:25,923
- And action.

1693
01:05:27,958 --> 01:05:29,460
- What's the matter?
You goin' home?

1694
01:05:31,295 --> 01:05:33,797
- That was a smart stunt
you tried to pull last night.

1695
01:05:33,830 --> 01:05:35,733
- What are you talking about?

1696
01:05:35,767 --> 01:05:36,733
- You think you can get away

1697
01:05:36,767 --> 01:05:38,002
with siccing
your floozies on Rose?

1698
01:05:38,035 --> 01:05:39,003
- I thought I might.

1699
01:05:39,037 --> 01:05:40,570
- How did you know about that?

1700
01:05:40,603 --> 01:05:42,807
- I saw the whole thing.
They tried to hang her.

1701
01:05:42,840 --> 01:05:43,840
- Where?

1702
01:05:43,873 --> 01:05:46,510
- Three miles out,
just off the main road.

1703
01:05:46,543 --> 01:05:48,145
- What were you doing there?

1704
01:05:48,178 --> 01:05:50,047
- That's none of
your business.

1705
01:05:50,080 --> 01:05:52,015
- That woman's my target
tomorrow,

1706
01:05:52,048 --> 01:05:54,152
and you're my hired help.

1707
01:05:54,185 --> 01:05:57,488
Everything you and she do
in this town is my business.

1708
01:05:57,522 --> 01:06:00,023
- Don't flatter yourself.

1709
01:06:00,057 --> 01:06:02,993
- I'll see you
in your room tonight.

1710
01:06:03,027 --> 01:06:04,995
- It's a hallway,
and don't you forget it!

1711
01:06:06,597 --> 01:06:07,865
- What it is,
little Wormy guy.

1712
01:06:09,133 --> 01:06:11,135
- I never liked him.

1713
01:06:11,168 --> 01:06:13,103
[♪♪♪]

1714
01:06:13,137 --> 01:06:14,105
- Pure maple syrup.

1715
01:06:14,138 --> 01:06:15,372
- Well, tomorrow's
the big day.

1716
01:06:15,405 --> 01:06:16,940
- Yeah, how are you?

1717
01:06:16,973 --> 01:06:18,442
- Yeah, big day.

1718
01:06:22,278 --> 01:06:24,282
- Right in the crouch,
he did it again.

1719
01:06:26,217 --> 01:06:30,120
- The man who shot
Vivian Valance.

1720
01:06:30,153 --> 01:06:32,155
- His hands are glowing.

1721
01:06:32,188 --> 01:06:35,092
[♪♪♪]

1722
01:06:35,125 --> 01:06:37,160
- Booze,
it's what's for dinner.

1723
01:06:37,193 --> 01:06:40,030
- The pertest marshal
of the old west.

1724
01:06:42,165 --> 01:06:44,102
- Why don't you do your
drinking in your room, Cane?

1725
01:06:44,135 --> 01:06:45,102
- It's a hall.

1726
01:06:45,135 --> 01:06:47,037
- Am I disturbing the peace?

1727
01:06:47,070 --> 01:06:50,407
I do disturb the peace
sometimes, you know.

1728
01:06:50,440 --> 01:06:52,710
- I was thinking of you.

1729
01:06:52,743 --> 01:06:55,512
- Well, don't be sorry,
beautiful.

1730
01:06:55,545 --> 01:06:58,348
Tonight's the last night
for you and me.

1731
01:06:58,382 --> 01:07:01,318
In the morning,
we'll both be done.

1732
01:07:01,352 --> 01:07:03,620
Go away.

1733
01:07:03,653 --> 01:07:06,123
- There are better ways
to say goodbye.

1734
01:07:06,157 --> 01:07:07,157
- Are there?

1735
01:07:07,190 --> 01:07:08,225
- Are you saying
what I think you're saying?

1736
01:07:08,258 --> 01:07:09,960
- Find the one
that feels good.

1737
01:07:11,862 --> 01:07:12,897
No.

1738
01:07:12,930 --> 01:07:14,865
- Ouch, there I go again.

1739
01:07:14,898 --> 01:07:15,833
- ...get out of here
and leave me alone.

1740
01:07:15,867 --> 01:07:17,300
I don't want to see you
or touch you

1741
01:07:17,333 --> 01:07:19,770
or ever remember
that I knew you.

1742
01:07:21,772 --> 01:07:22,807
- You don't mean that.

1743
01:07:25,342 --> 01:07:29,380
- More than anything I ever
meant in my life, I mean that.

1744
01:07:29,413 --> 01:07:31,115
- Except there was something
I said a while back

1745
01:07:31,148 --> 01:07:32,650
that I meant more than that.

1746
01:07:35,787 --> 01:07:38,588
- Hey, the angel of death
stopping by for a drink.

1747
01:07:38,622 --> 01:07:39,623
- Well, that's nice.

1748
01:07:41,025 --> 01:07:43,293
- Darn zipper, stuck again.

1749
01:07:48,298 --> 01:07:49,600
- Everything I need to know
I learn from booze.

1750
01:07:49,633 --> 01:07:50,768
[Knocking]

1751
01:07:50,802 --> 01:07:52,102
- Come out.

1752
01:07:55,940 --> 01:07:57,808
- Right in the middle of dinner.

1753
01:08:01,678 --> 01:08:05,048
- Okay. Who wants to come
into the hallway now?

1754
01:08:05,082 --> 01:08:08,052
- You're drunk.
- Come on in, Erica.

1755
01:08:08,085 --> 01:08:09,987
You and I are
on the same side.

1756
01:08:10,020 --> 01:08:11,155
- What are you talking about?

1757
01:08:11,188 --> 01:08:12,757
- We're humans, come here.

1758
01:08:16,093 --> 01:08:17,528
- Who cares what
I'm talking about?

1759
01:08:17,562 --> 01:08:19,130
- I should keep you drunk
all the time.

1760
01:08:19,163 --> 01:08:20,130
- I am.

1761
01:08:20,163 --> 01:08:21,932
We are. Something.

1762
01:08:23,267 --> 01:08:27,237
- Sneak, sneak,
sneak, sneak, squeak.

1763
01:08:29,973 --> 01:08:31,608
- You got brown eyes.

1764
01:08:31,642 --> 01:08:33,543
- You got a neck.

1765
01:08:35,178 --> 01:08:36,280
- I come here
to battle with you,

1766
01:08:36,313 --> 01:08:38,815
and you start talking
about the color of my eyes.

1767
01:08:38,848 --> 01:08:41,485
- You got the large pores
on your nose.

1768
01:08:44,120 --> 01:08:45,688
- You're a darn fool.

1769
01:08:45,722 --> 01:08:47,257
- That's what I am.

1770
01:08:47,290 --> 01:08:49,593
I'm a lovin' fool.

1771
01:08:49,627 --> 01:08:51,128
Lovin' fool.

1772
01:08:51,162 --> 01:08:53,797
- You taste like
Annie Greensprings.

1773
01:08:53,830 --> 01:08:55,833
- And you'll never stop?

1774
01:08:55,867 --> 01:08:58,202
- Who wants to sober up?

1775
01:08:58,235 --> 01:09:01,605
- Booze has made me
the king of the lovers.

1776
01:09:01,638 --> 01:09:02,940
- You're mean
when you're sober.

1777
01:09:02,973 --> 01:09:04,342
- I'm not mean.

1778
01:09:04,375 --> 01:09:07,512
I love everybody,
and especially I love Erica.

1779
01:09:07,545 --> 01:09:08,778
- ...a little theory
to put forth

1780
01:09:08,812 --> 01:09:11,548
about how he got around in
the back of the building there

1781
01:09:11,582 --> 01:09:13,683
and came around
in the horse and...

1782
01:09:21,358 --> 01:09:22,993
- But Tom,
that doesn't explain

1783
01:09:23,027 --> 01:09:25,395
how John Ireland could go
into the attorney's office

1784
01:09:25,428 --> 01:09:28,132
and suddenly appear
out of his horse in the street?

1785
01:09:28,165 --> 01:09:31,135
- Well Joel, it's really just
a broadened interpretation

1786
01:09:31,168 --> 01:09:33,103
of quantum linear
super positional.

1787
01:09:33,137 --> 01:09:34,272
- Super [indistinct].

1788
01:09:34,305 --> 01:09:36,140
They simply observe
the apparent relative state

1789
01:09:36,173 --> 01:09:38,775
of John Ireland in one place,
while in actuality

1790
01:09:38,808 --> 01:09:41,278
he coexisted
in the objective vector state.

1791
01:09:41,312 --> 01:09:42,278
You see?

1792
01:09:42,312 --> 01:09:43,247
- I have a theory.

1793
01:09:43,280 --> 01:09:44,448
That town is a bunch
of false fronts

1794
01:09:44,482 --> 01:09:46,050
and he ran over there
real fast.

1795
01:09:46,083 --> 01:09:47,183
- All right, I'm with him.

1796
01:09:47,217 --> 01:09:49,220
- Guys, why is it so hard
to accept the fact

1797
01:09:49,253 --> 01:09:51,555
that time is not an immutable
forward progression,

1798
01:09:51,588 --> 01:09:53,257
but a factor
in a space time model

1799
01:09:53,290 --> 01:09:56,393
of relativistic causality
and determinism, huh?

1800
01:09:56,427 --> 01:09:58,528
Guys? Hello?

1801
01:09:58,562 --> 01:09:59,763
Geez, like a couple
of cattle, these two.

1802
01:09:59,797 --> 01:10:00,830
Okay.
[CLEARS THROAT]

1803
01:10:00,863 --> 01:10:02,332
Here, I'm going
to demonstrate, okay?

1804
01:10:02,365 --> 01:10:03,933
Watch me closely.

1805
01:10:03,967 --> 01:10:06,403
Alle-oop!

1806
01:10:06,437 --> 01:10:07,537
- Hey wait, where did he go?

1807
01:10:07,572 --> 01:10:09,373
- I don't know.
- I'm over here, guys.

1808
01:10:09,407 --> 01:10:11,542
- Aaghh!
- I hate when you do that.

1809
01:10:11,575 --> 01:10:14,210
- Well, Servo, that's fantastic,
that's amazing.

1810
01:10:14,245 --> 01:10:16,180
- It's physics, Joel.
Plain and simple.

1811
01:10:16,213 --> 01:10:18,648
You just exist in one
observable region in space

1812
01:10:18,682 --> 01:10:22,587
and then zip, you simply realign
your point of origin, you see.

1813
01:10:22,620 --> 01:10:23,920
- He does this to me at nights.

1814
01:10:23,953 --> 01:10:25,022
Scares the hell out of me.

1815
01:10:25,055 --> 01:10:26,923
- It's easy, guys.
A trained chimp could do it.

1816
01:10:26,957 --> 01:10:28,192
Come on, Joel, try it.
It's fun.

1817
01:10:28,225 --> 01:10:31,495
- Okay, I'm going
to excite myself

1818
01:10:31,528 --> 01:10:36,200
in a position of undetermined
origin and region.

1819
01:10:36,233 --> 01:10:38,935
And...
- No, no, no.

1820
01:10:38,968 --> 01:10:40,603
- It didn't really work.
- Well, I'm not doing it.

1821
01:10:40,637 --> 01:10:43,373
Doing it makes you chubby
and stupid like Tom.

1822
01:10:43,407 --> 01:10:45,708
- Say it to my face.
- Joel, help me.

1823
01:10:45,742 --> 01:10:49,980
- Hey, do you know I can work
space and time this way? What?

1824
01:10:50,013 --> 01:10:52,582
- But that still doesn't explain
how John Ireland is able to go

1825
01:10:52,617 --> 01:10:55,818
into the attorney's office
and end up on his horse

1826
01:10:55,852 --> 01:10:58,888
on this, what the...

1827
01:10:58,922 --> 01:11:00,523
- See what I mean?

1828
01:11:00,557 --> 01:11:02,092
- Hey, my sandwich
is all again.

1829
01:11:02,125 --> 01:11:03,293
- Cool.

1830
01:11:03,327 --> 01:11:04,595
- We'll be right back.

1831
01:11:04,628 --> 01:11:05,595
- No, no, no, no.

1832
01:11:05,628 --> 01:11:07,732
They'll be right back,
we'll be right here.

1833
01:11:07,765 --> 01:11:08,932
- Honey, don't.

1834
01:11:08,965 --> 01:11:12,068
[♪♪♪]

1835
01:11:15,372 --> 01:11:16,673
- You mean that, Cane?
- Wow.

1836
01:11:16,707 --> 01:11:19,710
- 'Course I mean it.
I said it, didn't I?

1837
01:11:19,743 --> 01:11:22,647
- And to think I was worried
about poor little Rose.

1838
01:11:24,815 --> 01:11:26,417
- Magnussen,
puts her into the rods.

1839
01:11:26,450 --> 01:11:27,517
- Sorry.

1840
01:11:27,550 --> 01:11:28,518
- I have to tell you this...

1841
01:11:28,552 --> 01:11:29,520
- I'm Pops Staples.

1842
01:11:29,553 --> 01:11:30,687
- ...there's been
a change in plans.

1843
01:11:30,720 --> 01:11:32,455
- I'm going blonde.

1844
01:11:32,488 --> 01:11:33,623
- With the Mayor
locked up in jail,

1845
01:11:33,657 --> 01:11:35,158
we'll have to get the letter
from the Express rider

1846
01:11:35,192 --> 01:11:37,627
before he gets to town.

1847
01:11:37,662 --> 01:11:38,628
Do you understand?

1848
01:11:38,662 --> 01:11:39,897
- You got bright eyes.

1849
01:11:39,930 --> 01:11:41,398
- I understand.

1850
01:11:41,432 --> 01:11:45,968
- Then I'll meet you
at Immigrant Gap at 6:30.

1851
01:11:46,003 --> 01:11:48,172
- There are six immigrants
at the Gap.

1852
01:11:48,205 --> 01:11:50,173
- ...Immigrant Gap at 6:30.

1853
01:11:50,207 --> 01:11:52,208
- Booze. Write that down.

1854
01:11:55,978 --> 01:11:58,415
- Look, let's say hello.

1855
01:12:06,057 --> 01:12:07,323
- It's part of your love.

1856
01:12:09,660 --> 01:12:11,862
- Well, better do
next week's schedule.

1857
01:12:14,030 --> 01:12:15,132
- Boo!

1858
01:12:15,165 --> 01:12:18,068
- There!
You got what you asked for!

1859
01:12:18,068 --> 01:12:20,637
Don't go lying like that again
about how you love me.

1860
01:12:20,670 --> 01:12:21,638
[Gasping]

1861
01:12:21,672 --> 01:12:24,107
- I love you.

1862
01:12:24,140 --> 01:12:25,742
- I'm gonna kill you.

1863
01:12:25,775 --> 01:12:26,743
I'm gonna kill you

1864
01:12:26,777 --> 01:12:28,678
but I'm gonna use
Cane Miro to do it.

1865
01:12:28,712 --> 01:12:29,880
All I have to do is pay him,

1866
01:12:29,913 --> 01:12:33,117
and he'll follow you
to the end of the world.

1867
01:12:33,150 --> 01:12:34,350
- You won't do that.

1868
01:12:34,385 --> 01:12:36,187
- Missus, lady, don't, no.

1869
01:12:36,220 --> 01:12:38,688
- Jake,
you're a dead little man.

1870
01:12:38,722 --> 01:12:41,692
Now get out
before I kill you myself.

1871
01:12:41,725 --> 01:12:43,793
- Okay, what time do you
want me in tomorrow?

1872
01:12:46,130 --> 01:12:48,398
- This is just like Silverado,
only better.

1873
01:12:48,432 --> 01:12:49,433
- Yeah, a lot better.

1874
01:12:51,268 --> 01:12:55,305
- Joel, I hate
my pause so bad.

1875
01:13:06,617 --> 01:13:07,818
- Oh, sail at hurrah.

1876
01:13:12,022 --> 01:13:12,990
- Don't shoot me!

1877
01:13:13,023 --> 01:13:15,025
- No, no, I'm the comic, really.
Don't shoot.

1878
01:13:15,058 --> 01:13:16,727
- What do you mean by
bursting in here like that?

1879
01:13:16,760 --> 01:13:19,028
- You'd better go
after Cane Miro.

1880
01:13:19,062 --> 01:13:20,563
- Cane? What for?

1881
01:13:20,597 --> 01:13:22,232
- He's gonna shoot you.

1882
01:13:22,265 --> 01:13:23,867
- Over my dead body.

1883
01:13:23,900 --> 01:13:25,068
- How do you know that?

1884
01:13:25,102 --> 01:13:27,037
- Erica Page hired him
to do it.

1885
01:13:27,070 --> 01:13:29,238
I seen her pay him
$3,000 to kill you

1886
01:13:29,272 --> 01:13:31,275
if the railroad
don't come through Oracle.

1887
01:13:31,308 --> 01:13:32,508
- The railroad?

1888
01:13:32,542 --> 01:13:34,612
- They're just waiting for you
to read that letter, Mayor.

1889
01:13:34,645 --> 01:13:36,980
- I'd better go after him.
- No. No. You can't.

1890
01:13:37,013 --> 01:13:38,682
You can't arrest a man
on Jake's hearsay.

1891
01:13:38,715 --> 01:13:39,917
- You'd better do it!

1892
01:13:39,950 --> 01:13:41,017
- I emphatically agree.

1893
01:13:41,050 --> 01:13:42,718
- We just have to wait
until he does something.

1894
01:13:42,752 --> 01:13:44,387
- By then,
it may be too late.

1895
01:13:44,420 --> 01:13:47,057
- So Erica paid him.

1896
01:13:47,090 --> 01:13:49,058
She's probably behind
Scott's killing too.

1897
01:13:49,092 --> 01:13:51,562
- Sure she is, and she's
in love with Cane Miro

1898
01:13:51,595 --> 01:13:52,528
and him with her too.

1899
01:13:52,562 --> 01:13:54,665
- Can I hit you, Wormy,
just once?

1900
01:13:54,698 --> 01:13:56,400
- Go on, Jake.

1901
01:13:56,433 --> 01:13:57,767
- You mean, you're gonna
let him be?

1902
01:13:57,802 --> 01:13:58,802
- Speaking words of wisdom.

1903
01:13:58,835 --> 01:14:00,837
- Yeah, that's right.
Goodnight, little man.

1904
01:14:00,870 --> 01:14:04,140
- Don't call me that!
Erica called me that.

1905
01:14:04,173 --> 01:14:05,142
I don't like it!

1906
01:14:05,175 --> 01:14:06,108
- Touched a nerve.

1907
01:14:06,143 --> 01:14:07,210
- I don't like it!

1908
01:14:07,243 --> 01:14:08,578
- We are kind of little.

1909
01:14:10,313 --> 01:14:11,648
- You're making
a big mistake, Rose.

1910
01:14:11,682 --> 01:14:12,782
- Stay in school.

1911
01:14:12,817 --> 01:14:14,350
- You ought to
move in tonight.

1912
01:14:14,383 --> 01:14:16,018
- On what evidence?

1913
01:14:16,052 --> 01:14:18,188
Jake's testimony?

1914
01:14:18,222 --> 01:14:20,223
Any cheap lawyer would prove
him mentally incompetent

1915
01:14:20,257 --> 01:14:21,225
in three minutes.

1916
01:14:21,258 --> 01:14:23,327
- Hey, they haven't
invented that yet.

1917
01:14:24,628 --> 01:14:29,198
- No, we-- we just
have to wait it out.

1918
01:14:29,232 --> 01:14:31,835
- Why am I such a misfit?

1919
01:14:32,802 --> 01:14:33,803
- Evening, Jake.

1920
01:14:35,338 --> 01:14:36,373
- I'm a pixie.

1921
01:14:39,910 --> 01:14:42,545
- Don't say hi. Come on.

1922
01:14:43,680 --> 01:14:44,782
- Oh, for crying out loud,
he's upset.

1923
01:14:44,815 --> 01:14:47,783
Little, what's got into you?
Little, honey?

1924
01:14:49,920 --> 01:14:52,155
[♪♪♪]

1925
01:14:52,188 --> 01:14:54,290
There, you see.

1926
01:14:54,323 --> 01:14:55,292
- He did it again.

1927
01:14:55,325 --> 01:14:56,293
- So they're just
incorporating the fact

1928
01:14:56,327 --> 01:14:58,228
that they're just
close friends.

1929
01:14:59,963 --> 01:15:01,532
- He's everywhere.
- He's everywhere.

1930
01:15:07,437 --> 01:15:09,607
- Little, honey,
did I say something?

1931
01:15:11,375 --> 01:15:13,877
- I don't think Little Man
is cut out for a rampage.

1932
01:15:13,910 --> 01:15:17,047
[♪♪♪]

1933
01:15:20,617 --> 01:15:23,453
- Wait, I'm the little man.
Ow, owee!

1934
01:15:23,487 --> 01:15:26,457
[♪♪♪]

1935
01:15:27,490 --> 01:15:29,827
- Now, what were you
so mad about?

1936
01:15:31,395 --> 01:15:33,763
- Should I taunt him?
Nah, he's dead.

1937
01:15:37,067 --> 01:15:39,703
- Oh hi, just cleaning up
down here.

1938
01:15:39,737 --> 01:15:41,838
Look what I found.
Does he belong to you?

1939
01:15:46,577 --> 01:15:49,780
- Oh, good, you killed
the little man.

1940
01:15:49,813 --> 01:15:51,415
- Am I in time for happy hour?

1941
01:15:51,448 --> 01:15:53,550
I could really go for
some [indistinct].

1942
01:15:57,620 --> 01:16:01,190
- You're standing
on his head, dear.

1943
01:16:01,223 --> 01:16:03,660
- How'd it happen?

1944
01:16:03,693 --> 01:16:06,297
- Jake tried to kill him.

1945
01:16:06,330 --> 01:16:07,397
It was self-defense.

1946
01:16:07,430 --> 01:16:10,900
- And thus the first
L.A. cop was born.

1947
01:16:10,933 --> 01:16:12,802
- That right?

1948
01:16:12,835 --> 01:16:14,070
- You might call it that.

1949
01:16:21,412 --> 01:16:23,013
- She's quiet today.

1950
01:16:25,048 --> 01:16:26,015
- Oh goodness, yes.

1951
01:16:26,048 --> 01:16:27,217
I always come here on Saturday

1952
01:16:27,250 --> 01:16:29,820
because they have that
really nice breakfast burrito.

1953
01:16:29,853 --> 01:16:30,820
You know, you take eggs and...

1954
01:16:30,853 --> 01:16:31,955
- What time is it?

1955
01:16:31,988 --> 01:16:34,223
- It's about 7:15,
like the last time you asked me.

1956
01:16:34,257 --> 01:16:36,158
- Take it easy, Gideon.

1957
01:16:36,192 --> 01:16:37,962
- Did you see Cane Miro
this morning?

1958
01:16:37,995 --> 01:16:39,797
- Not yet.

1959
01:16:39,830 --> 01:16:41,332
- You're hard to talk to.

1960
01:16:41,365 --> 01:16:42,832
- You feel up to meeting
the mail at eight?

1961
01:16:42,865 --> 01:16:45,002
- No, but thanks for asking.

1962
01:16:45,035 --> 01:16:46,970
- My duty.

1963
01:16:47,003 --> 01:16:49,973
I failed it once.

1964
01:16:50,007 --> 01:16:51,508
I won't again.

1965
01:16:51,542 --> 01:16:53,343
- Probably they will,
but let's go.

1966
01:16:53,343 --> 01:16:54,410
- Wait 'til they get here.

1967
01:16:54,443 --> 01:16:56,447
No sense muffing it
with a miss at long range.

1968
01:16:56,480 --> 01:16:57,680
- You didn't pay for this.
It's your shot.

1969
01:16:57,713 --> 01:17:00,183
- Hey, she got her horse
reupholstered.

1970
01:17:03,620 --> 01:17:05,322
- Hi, folks!

1971
01:17:05,355 --> 01:17:07,123
What brings you
way out here so early?

1972
01:17:07,157 --> 01:17:08,225
- Have you got
the railroad letter?

1973
01:17:08,258 --> 01:17:10,160
- Right here,
safe and sound.

1974
01:17:10,193 --> 01:17:11,928
- That's nice to know.

1975
01:17:11,962 --> 01:17:13,063
Hand it over.

1976
01:17:13,097 --> 01:17:15,498
- You guys sign for it.

1977
01:17:15,532 --> 01:17:18,102
- There's postage due
on that letter, ma'am.

1978
01:17:18,135 --> 01:17:20,903
- Which one of you
gets the Starlog?

1979
01:17:20,937 --> 01:17:21,938
- Now get out of here.

1980
01:17:23,607 --> 01:17:24,942
- You're welcome.

1981
01:17:27,243 --> 01:17:28,212
- Put near the switch.

1982
01:17:28,245 --> 01:17:29,645
Someone's shooting
the postal worker.

1983
01:17:31,247 --> 01:17:33,617
- Mom wants to stay with us,
so see for yourself.

1984
01:17:33,650 --> 01:17:34,583
- Not coming.

1985
01:17:34,617 --> 01:17:37,820
- Yeah, but Peg
sends her regards.

1986
01:17:37,853 --> 01:17:40,657
- They're going to build
the line through Rockdale.

1987
01:17:40,690 --> 01:17:41,658
- That's a shame.

1988
01:17:41,692 --> 01:17:43,660
- Is that all you can say?

1989
01:17:43,693 --> 01:17:44,695
- It's a darn shame.

1990
01:17:44,728 --> 01:17:46,463
- That's better.

1991
01:17:46,497 --> 01:17:49,265
- You ready to help me
knock over the town?

1992
01:17:49,298 --> 01:17:50,600
- I've got two bullets,

1993
01:17:50,633 --> 01:17:53,937
one for Rose,
and one for Captain Polk.

1994
01:17:55,105 --> 01:17:57,407
- Hey, she's littering.
There's a fine for that.

1995
01:17:59,642 --> 01:18:01,412
- Nine o'clock.

1996
01:18:01,445 --> 01:18:03,647
It's never been
this late before.

1997
01:18:03,680 --> 01:18:05,948
- Something's happened to him.

1998
01:18:05,982 --> 01:18:07,650
- Think I'd better ride out
and meet him.

1999
01:18:07,683 --> 01:18:09,418
- No, you'd better stay here.

2000
01:18:09,452 --> 01:18:10,753
- You go on, Rose.

2001
01:18:10,787 --> 01:18:12,622
Gideon and I can take
care of ourselves.

2002
01:18:12,655 --> 01:18:15,525
- Speak for yourself, honey.

2003
01:18:15,558 --> 01:18:18,095
Oklahoma [indistinct].

2004
01:18:18,128 --> 01:18:19,462
- Move away, folks.

2005
01:18:19,495 --> 01:18:21,632
Might be some shootin'
around here.

2006
01:18:21,665 --> 01:18:24,567
Nobody on the street except
Fred, Mayor Polk, and me.

2007
01:18:24,600 --> 01:18:27,170
- Well, that's pretty much
the whole town, pretty much.

2008
01:18:28,972 --> 01:18:31,073
- Ouch, ouch, ouch!

2009
01:18:32,542 --> 01:18:34,710
- ♪ Who cares [indistinct]
He's got... ♪

2010
01:18:34,745 --> 01:18:36,613
[Dog barking]

2011
01:18:43,753 --> 01:18:46,590
- Tell Sergeant [indistinct]
it's the hot cowies.

2012
01:18:46,623 --> 01:18:49,358
- Well, look, they have
a photo man over there.

2013
01:18:49,392 --> 01:18:52,262
- Yeah.
And it's saying, oh, no.

2014
01:18:54,230 --> 01:18:55,498
- Army man.

2015
01:18:55,532 --> 01:18:57,800
- Joshua!
- Light show.

2016
01:18:57,833 --> 01:18:59,837
- Looks like he means
to hold us up.

2017
01:18:59,870 --> 01:19:01,672
- You'll have to get him.

2018
01:19:01,705 --> 01:19:03,207
- You'll have to pay for it.

2019
01:19:03,240 --> 01:19:05,542
- I gave you 3,000 to take care
of the law in Oracle.

2020
01:19:05,575 --> 01:19:06,543
He's part of it.

2021
01:19:06,577 --> 01:19:07,543
- So you did.

2022
01:19:07,577 --> 01:19:09,345
I should've listened
to the fine print.

2023
01:19:09,378 --> 01:19:10,613
Take the other side
of the road.

2024
01:19:11,882 --> 01:19:12,882
- That's far enough!

2025
01:19:16,620 --> 01:19:17,987
- They shot Joshua!

2026
01:19:18,020 --> 01:19:22,558
- They didn't shoot the sheriff,
but they did shoot the deputy.

2027
01:19:23,960 --> 01:19:25,862
- Haven't even got a gun
to protect myself with.

2028
01:19:25,895 --> 01:19:27,463
- I'm losing power, captain.

2029
01:19:27,497 --> 01:19:28,632
- I'll find you one.

2030
01:19:28,665 --> 01:19:31,635
- Then put on another pair
of pants, for God's sakes.

2031
01:19:31,668 --> 01:19:33,137
- Where are you going?

2032
01:19:33,170 --> 01:19:34,805
- After something
to shoot with.

2033
01:19:34,838 --> 01:19:35,872
- You better stay here.

2034
01:19:37,440 --> 01:19:39,242
That won't do you any good.

2035
01:19:39,275 --> 01:19:41,712
- Gideon asked for a gun
and I aim to find one for him.

2036
01:19:41,745 --> 01:19:44,213
- But there's a ten-day
waiting period.

2037
01:19:44,247 --> 01:19:45,948
- I'm afraid it's too late.

2038
01:19:50,053 --> 01:19:52,722
- Hey, could you tell us
where the Red Dog Saloon is?

2039
01:19:52,755 --> 01:19:53,923
- Where's the Marshal?
- She rode out of town.

2040
01:19:56,158 --> 01:19:57,793
- What's the matter?

2041
01:19:57,827 --> 01:19:59,963
- Thought I saw an old friend.

2042
01:19:59,997 --> 01:20:00,997
- Oops, got to pee.

2043
01:20:01,030 --> 01:20:02,632
- You haven't got time
for the Mayor.

2044
01:20:02,665 --> 01:20:04,467
- I waited 15 years.

2045
01:20:04,500 --> 01:20:06,503
This is as good a time as any
to settle it.

2046
01:20:09,973 --> 01:20:11,107
- He's after you!

2047
01:20:13,042 --> 01:20:14,912
Get out of sight!

2048
01:20:14,945 --> 01:20:16,680
- Wow, guess he's not
the hero anymore.

2049
01:20:19,682 --> 01:20:20,650
- Felicity!

2050
01:20:20,683 --> 01:20:22,385
- I said here is
a beef roast in the...

2051
01:20:22,418 --> 01:20:25,422
- Yes, where?
- Don't be afraid for me.

2052
01:20:25,455 --> 01:20:27,523
Just get yourself
away from here.

2053
01:20:27,557 --> 01:20:28,825
- Retired of Florida.

2054
01:20:28,858 --> 01:20:34,030
- You don't have to be brave
just for some no-account killer.

2055
01:20:34,063 --> 01:20:35,432
- Got you out to school norm.

2056
01:20:35,465 --> 01:20:36,498
- Sorry.

2057
01:20:39,302 --> 01:20:40,937
- You were supposed to
get me a gun.

2058
01:20:40,970 --> 01:20:42,505
- Great. What's next?

2059
01:20:42,538 --> 01:20:43,640
- Oh-oh!

2060
01:20:45,042 --> 01:20:47,843
- Drive.
I mean, wait a sec.

2061
01:20:50,080 --> 01:20:52,882
- Could we maybe settle this
with a game of Scrabble?

2062
01:20:55,552 --> 01:20:56,987
- American Gothic, Solo.

2063
01:21:00,623 --> 01:21:02,892
- Got chores.

2064
01:21:02,925 --> 01:21:03,893
- The hell.

2065
01:21:07,897 --> 01:21:09,365
- I'm sorry,
you look too stupid.

2066
01:21:09,398 --> 01:21:10,433
I can't shoot you.

2067
01:21:13,470 --> 01:21:15,405
- Bad idea.
Fork thing didn't work.

2068
01:21:18,875 --> 01:21:21,978
- Oh hi, honey, gee,
I've had a very productive day.

2069
01:21:22,012 --> 01:21:24,047
- Alright, you've taken care
of your personal pleasure.

2070
01:21:24,080 --> 01:21:25,682
Rose will be here any minute.

2071
01:21:25,715 --> 01:21:26,717
- It's your show.

2072
01:21:26,750 --> 01:21:27,783
- Right.

2073
01:21:27,817 --> 01:21:29,585
- Ride to the alley
by your hotel and wait.

2074
01:21:29,618 --> 01:21:31,655
I'll be in the Land Office
across the street.

2075
01:21:31,688 --> 01:21:32,888
- Who's this guy?

2076
01:21:32,922 --> 01:21:35,325
- You drag these bodies up
to the street by the alley.

2077
01:21:35,358 --> 01:21:37,093
When Rose gets here,
keep her talking.

2078
01:21:37,127 --> 01:21:38,962
If you give us away,
I'll kill you.

2079
01:21:38,995 --> 01:21:40,130
- Anything you say.

2080
01:21:40,163 --> 01:21:41,832
- Get moving!

2081
01:21:41,865 --> 01:21:42,832
- You plan fast.

2082
01:21:42,865 --> 01:21:44,200
- I always have.

2083
01:21:44,233 --> 01:21:45,702
I'll signal you
when to take her.

2084
01:21:45,735 --> 01:21:47,970
- You seem really crabby today.

2085
01:21:51,340 --> 01:21:53,910
- Oh, you leave town
and your work piles up.

2086
01:21:55,478 --> 01:21:57,647
- Yeah, I don't like being
the sheriff anymore.

2087
01:22:02,985 --> 01:22:03,987
- Should be two Ls.

2088
01:22:05,288 --> 01:22:06,990
- One's okay.

2089
01:22:07,023 --> 01:22:08,392
- Oh, hi, everyone's dead.

2090
01:22:08,425 --> 01:22:09,758
- They got the Mayor,
and his wife too.

2091
01:22:12,162 --> 01:22:14,530
Killers rode out of town.

2092
01:22:14,563 --> 01:22:15,732
- Nooner, come on.

2093
01:22:15,765 --> 01:22:16,967
- No, not now.

2094
01:22:17,000 --> 01:22:18,435
- Did you see who they were?

2095
01:22:18,468 --> 01:22:20,137
- No, I didn't get
a good look.

2096
01:22:21,638 --> 01:22:22,872
- Nooner, you know.

2097
01:22:22,905 --> 01:22:24,240
- No. What do you mean?

2098
01:22:24,273 --> 01:22:26,408
- Are you sure it wasn't
Cane Miro and Erica?

2099
01:22:26,442 --> 01:22:28,110
- Oh, I'm positive it wasn't.

2100
01:22:29,745 --> 01:22:30,780
- Come on.

2101
01:22:30,813 --> 01:22:31,815
- There's no time.

2102
01:22:35,585 --> 01:22:37,420
- There is no nooner now.

2103
01:22:40,957 --> 01:22:41,958
- It's alright.

2104
01:22:43,793 --> 01:22:45,595
- That's why I couldn't talk.

2105
01:22:45,628 --> 01:22:46,628
- Who am I after?

2106
01:22:46,663 --> 01:22:48,163
- Cane Miro.

2107
01:22:49,398 --> 01:22:50,333
- Thanks.

2108
01:22:50,367 --> 01:22:52,302
- Remind me to kill you later.

2109
01:22:52,335 --> 01:22:55,838
[♪♪♪]

2110
01:22:55,872 --> 01:23:01,812
- Robby, Steve,
Donny, Uncle Charlie...

2111
01:23:06,983 --> 01:23:08,183
- And there's jeep tracks
on there.

2112
01:23:08,217 --> 01:23:09,752
- I thought so.

2113
01:23:12,022 --> 01:23:13,390
- This film makes
"Dirty Dingus Magee"

2114
01:23:13,423 --> 01:23:15,092
look like a John Ford film.

2115
01:23:18,227 --> 01:23:21,698
- The 12 days
and the Baja 500.

2116
01:23:21,732 --> 01:23:23,833
- Are they circling each other?

2117
01:23:27,303 --> 01:23:29,538
- Now he's in Ireland.
What's going on?

2118
01:23:32,642 --> 01:23:34,077
- From Vermont to New Mexico.

2119
01:23:36,913 --> 01:23:39,448
- Power assisted
anti-lock coops.

2120
01:23:43,018 --> 01:23:45,222
- Well, I'll just follow
these jeep tracks.

2121
01:23:52,862 --> 01:23:55,298
- Guys, that's a baobab tree.

2122
01:23:55,332 --> 01:23:56,733
Now they're in the Serengeti.

2123
01:24:02,172 --> 01:24:04,940
- Man, I wish there was
a pump and lunch around here.

2124
01:24:10,413 --> 01:24:13,282
- Tonight, on a very special
Guns of Will Sonnett...

2125
01:24:16,853 --> 01:24:17,820
[Gun cocks]

2126
01:24:20,790 --> 01:24:23,092
- Unlike all the horses
so affected by the gunfire.

2127
01:24:24,293 --> 01:24:26,595
- Nice day
for shooting rabbits.

2128
01:24:26,628 --> 01:24:29,665
- Actually it's better
when it's a little over catch.

2129
01:24:29,698 --> 01:24:31,600
- That any way to talk
to an old friend?

2130
01:24:31,633 --> 01:24:32,602
- Don't be a fool, Cane.

2131
01:24:32,635 --> 01:24:34,203
I'm out here to kill you.

2132
01:24:34,237 --> 01:24:35,938
- I wouldn't do that to you.

2133
01:24:35,972 --> 01:24:37,173
- I know you've been
missing on purpose,

2134
01:24:37,207 --> 01:24:38,808
but it won't do you any good.

2135
01:24:38,842 --> 01:24:41,010
- You know, they should start
dating other people.

2136
01:24:41,043 --> 01:24:42,045
- Yes, or shooting.

2137
01:24:42,078 --> 01:24:44,313
- You haven't been doing
so well yourself.

2138
01:24:44,347 --> 01:24:45,548
- I think maybe
they should read,

2139
01:24:45,582 --> 01:24:47,383
you guys don't understand.

2140
01:24:48,885 --> 01:24:50,887
- Back in those days, when
you had relationship problems,

2141
01:24:50,920 --> 01:24:52,955
you really had
relationship problems.

2142
01:24:53,957 --> 01:24:55,625
- That hurts.

2143
01:24:55,658 --> 01:24:57,260
- Rose?

2144
01:24:57,293 --> 01:24:58,862
- Yes?

2145
01:24:58,895 --> 01:25:00,095
- In case I don't see you again,

2146
01:25:00,130 --> 01:25:02,465
there's something
I'd like to ask you.

2147
01:25:02,498 --> 01:25:03,800
- What is it?

2148
01:25:03,833 --> 01:25:04,767
- Do you...

2149
01:25:04,800 --> 01:25:06,502
- What's Fred MacMurray like?

2150
01:25:06,535 --> 01:25:08,572
- ...is it a two-way thing?

2151
01:25:08,605 --> 01:25:12,008
- He just popped
that out of his shoulder.

2152
01:25:12,042 --> 01:25:13,175
- It's a two-way thing.

2153
01:25:16,512 --> 01:25:18,080
- Okay, it's kind of
one way now.

2154
01:25:23,352 --> 01:25:24,653
- This is kind of like
Ride the High Country,

2155
01:25:24,687 --> 01:25:26,588
except it's not very cool.

2156
01:25:34,830 --> 01:25:37,667
- You shoot like a girl.

2157
01:25:37,700 --> 01:25:38,968
- Rose?

2158
01:25:39,002 --> 01:25:40,537
- Hey, you don't have eight
or nine pounds of plasma on you.

2159
01:25:40,570 --> 01:25:41,670
Do you?

2160
01:25:44,373 --> 01:25:45,408
- Rose?

2161
01:25:45,442 --> 01:25:47,843
- Come on, pick up.
I know you're there.

2162
01:25:52,215 --> 01:25:54,183
- Oh, right in the hat.

2163
01:26:00,890 --> 01:26:03,527
- I know I'm going to
have to make new friends.

2164
01:26:05,093 --> 01:26:06,762
- She'll have to start
a new town now.

2165
01:26:06,795 --> 01:26:07,797
- Yeah.

2166
01:26:14,603 --> 01:26:18,307
- What did you mean by
that two-way thing?

2167
01:26:21,377 --> 01:26:23,245
- God, the paperwork!

2168
01:26:25,482 --> 01:26:27,750
- Well, now she's
the sheriff of herself.

2169
01:26:27,783 --> 01:26:30,587
- Yeah, well, at least
the horses are cheap now.

2170
01:26:33,723 --> 01:26:34,690
- You found him.

2171
01:26:34,723 --> 01:26:36,458
- We cancelled crazy days.

2172
01:26:36,492 --> 01:26:38,293
- Yeah.

2173
01:26:38,327 --> 01:26:39,695
Here.

2174
01:26:39,728 --> 01:26:40,963
- Look, before we say
anything else,

2175
01:26:40,997 --> 01:26:42,732
are you setting me up here?

2176
01:26:42,765 --> 01:26:44,700
- By the twisted tree
near Boot Hill.

2177
01:26:44,733 --> 01:26:47,970
- Oh, I love that place.

2178
01:26:48,003 --> 01:26:48,972
- Jimmy, I'm leaving Oracle.

2179
01:26:49,005 --> 01:26:51,640
I don't want to see
this place again.

2180
01:26:51,673 --> 01:26:53,042
When the new marshal
gets here...

2181
01:26:54,543 --> 01:26:55,612
give him this.

2182
01:26:55,645 --> 01:26:57,180
- You don't need a Marshall.
There's no people.

2183
01:26:57,213 --> 01:26:59,282
- My things are packed.
They're in the Marshal's Office.

2184
01:26:59,315 --> 01:27:00,717
- Give him all my bras too.

2185
01:27:00,750 --> 01:27:02,452
- ...the way for me?

2186
01:27:02,485 --> 01:27:05,187
- Be glad to, Mrs. Hood.

2187
01:27:05,220 --> 01:27:07,857
- Thanks.
- By the way, who are you?

2188
01:27:07,890 --> 01:27:11,360
- And don't set up
the new Marshall.

2189
01:27:11,393 --> 01:27:12,495
- Welcome back, everybody.

2190
01:27:12,528 --> 01:27:13,697
- We're all done.

2191
01:27:13,730 --> 01:27:15,298
- Oh, that's right.

2192
01:27:20,002 --> 01:27:21,370
- Hi, I'm here to kill you.

2193
01:27:23,305 --> 01:27:26,175
- 'Scuse me, Ma'am.
My name is Sam Bass.

2194
01:27:26,208 --> 01:27:30,313
Can you tell me where I might
find the Marshal's Office?

2195
01:27:30,347 --> 01:27:31,313
- Right down there.

2196
01:27:31,347 --> 01:27:33,415
- Third body on the left.
- Thanks, Ma'am.

2197
01:27:33,448 --> 01:27:35,885
Looks like a nice, quiet
little town you've got here.

2198
01:27:35,918 --> 01:27:37,220
- Dead quiet.

2199
01:27:37,253 --> 01:27:39,222
- Yeah.

2200
01:27:39,255 --> 01:27:40,357
Yeah, it is.

2201
01:27:40,390 --> 01:27:41,958
- Except for all the bodies.

2202
01:27:41,992 --> 01:27:45,093
[♪♪♪]

2203
01:27:46,862 --> 01:27:49,698
- ♪ All that glitters is gold ♪

2204
01:27:51,233 --> 01:27:53,235
Hey lady, you forget
to tell me where the...

2205
01:27:53,268 --> 01:27:54,237
Oh, my God.

2206
01:27:54,270 --> 01:27:55,938
[Screams]

2207
01:27:57,940 --> 01:27:59,942
- Oh, boy!
That was terrible.

2208
01:28:13,222 --> 01:28:14,590
- You know,
Roger Corman can make

2209
01:28:14,623 --> 01:28:15,958
almost anything seem dismal,

2210
01:28:15,992 --> 01:28:19,762
but still, the 1870s
had to be a pretty foul decade.

2211
01:28:19,795 --> 01:28:21,830
- Well, I barely
remember the 1870s,

2212
01:28:21,863 --> 01:28:23,767
but I was around
for the 1970s

2213
01:28:23,800 --> 01:28:26,568
and you're right,
it was a pretty foul decade.

2214
01:28:26,602 --> 01:28:27,637
- Cool, well.

2215
01:28:27,670 --> 01:28:29,505
Did you wear chaps
and cowboy hats and stuff?

2216
01:28:29,538 --> 01:28:31,773
- No, but I knew people who did.

2217
01:28:31,807 --> 01:28:33,175
- Were the James gang
still around?

2218
01:28:33,208 --> 01:28:35,978
- Oh no, Joel Walsh broke up
and started Barnstorm.

2219
01:28:36,012 --> 01:28:37,647
- Oh, hey,
did you ever rope cattle

2220
01:28:37,680 --> 01:28:39,548
or bust bronchos and stuff?

2221
01:28:39,582 --> 01:28:42,252
- No, but I rolled
my charger a few times.

2222
01:28:42,285 --> 01:28:43,452
- Wow.

2223
01:28:43,485 --> 01:28:45,988
- Well, let's read some letters,
where are they?

2224
01:28:46,022 --> 01:28:47,023
- Yeah, they're down there.

2225
01:28:47,057 --> 01:28:49,792
- Yup, fresh, crispy letters.

2226
01:28:49,825 --> 01:28:52,695
- Hey, they are fresh
and crispy.

2227
01:28:52,728 --> 01:28:54,963
- Yeah, we deep-fried 'em.

2228
01:28:54,997 --> 01:28:56,332
- Why?

2229
01:28:56,365 --> 01:28:58,233
- I'm not sure I understand
the question, Joel.

2230
01:28:58,267 --> 01:29:02,105
- Never mind, let's just
blot out some of the grease,

2231
01:29:02,138 --> 01:29:05,808
break up some of this admittedly
delicious looking crust,

2232
01:29:05,842 --> 01:29:07,643
ooh, caution,
filling is hot.

2233
01:29:07,677 --> 01:29:09,645
- Careful.
- Okay.

2234
01:29:09,678 --> 01:29:11,913
And about how many weeks'
worth of letters

2235
01:29:11,947 --> 01:29:13,548
did you fry anyway?

2236
01:29:13,582 --> 01:29:16,418
- Well, like four mail bags
full, whatever that is.

2237
01:29:16,452 --> 01:29:17,653
- Yup.

2238
01:29:17,687 --> 01:29:19,688
- We had a lot of frying medium
and a lot of letters.

2239
01:29:19,722 --> 01:29:22,692
- Well, honeys, don't deep fry
anymore letters, okay?

2240
01:29:22,725 --> 01:29:23,993
